4 maps to immune→gentle→threshold→smooth→catastrophic\nTable 3 (OpenVLA pruning): k=1.0: 74%, k=0.95: 69.4%, k=0.9: 62%, k=0.7: 49%, k=0.5: 37.4%, k=0.3: 20.6%\nTable 4 (X-VLA): k=1.0: 99.2%, k=0.9: 97.6%, k=0.7: 56.2% (cliff), k=0.5: 42.6%, k=0.3: 23%\nTable 5 (RoboFlamingo): Baseline 42%, k=0.7: 28%, k=0.5: 15%, k=0.3: 4%\nTable 6 (SmolVLA VTR M-sweep): All M values within ±5pp, M=32: 60.1±1.3%, flat M-curve\nTable 7 (VTR universality): SmolVLA M=32: 60.1±1.3% vs 0% hard prune; OpenVLA M=32: 45% vs ~8%; M=64: 52% vs 20.6%; M=96: 58% vs 37.4%\nTable 8 (L2-norm vs random): max |Δ|=2.0pp across all k values\nTable 9 (Seed robustness): X-VLA k=0.7 seed42: 56.2% vs seed44: 52.0% (Δ=-4.2pp); OpenVLA k=0.7: 49% vs 47% (Δ=-2pp)\n\n## Imagined Experiments from Design\nThe experimental design includes 12 imagined experiments. The paper covers:\n- OpenVLA VTC-only M=32/64/96 (3 experiments) ✓\n- OpenVLA L2-norm pruning at k=0.95/0.9/0.7/0.5/0.3 (5 experiments) ✓\n- X-VLA seed44 replication at k=0.7 and k=0.9 (2 experiments) ✓\n- OpenVLA random pruning seed43 at k=0.7 and k=0.5 (2 experiments) ✓\n- RoboFlamingo Perceiver Resampler baseline + pruning (1 experiment) — partially covered (pruning curve shown but noted as bonus)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Methodology Assessment\n- Is the experimental methodology sound? Are the evaluation protocols appropriate?\n- Is the pruning protocol (true removal vs masking) well justified?\n- Is the CV diagnostic metric well-motivated and rigorously validated?\n- Is VTR's design (cross-attention re-encoding) well-justified against alternatives?\n- Are there methodological concerns (e.g., different eval modes for different models: offline for π0 and OFT, closed-loop for others)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are sample sizes sufficient (500 episodes per config)?\n- Is the seed robustness analysis adequate (only 2 seeds for a few configs)?\n- Are confidence intervals and statistical tests reported appropriately?\n- Are the VTR results convincing (3 seeds for SmolVLA, but single seed for OpenVLA VTC)?\n- Is the comparison fair across models with very different baselines (π0 99.2% vs RoboFlamingo 42%)?\n\n### 3. Completeness and Coverage\n- Does the paper cover the planned experiments adequately?\n- Are there missing ablations or experiments that would strengthen claims?\n- Is the RoboFlamingo experiment convincingly presented?\n\n### 4. Specific Concerns\n- π0 is evaluated offline (normalized L1 loss) not closed-loop. Is this a significant limitation?\n- RoboFlamingo baseline is 42% — does this low baseline limit interpretability?\n- The paper claims VTR is \"universal\" but only tests on 2 connector types\n- VTR adds 22M parameters and requires fine-tuning — how does this compare to parameter-free alternatives?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)  \n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Methods Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Experiments Score** (1-10, with justification)","uuid":"159af27e-8040-4476-a06a-44f1bf01eb50","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents a systematic cross-architecture study of visual token pruning robustness across 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families. Key contributions:\n1. A four-class taxonomy of pruning behavior: immune (π0, pure linear), gentle graded (RoboFlamingo, Perceiver Resampler), threshold-graded (X-VLA, linear+BART), smooth graded (OpenVLA, MLP), catastrophic (SmolVLA, pixel-shuffle).\n2. Importance CV as a zero-cost diagnostic predictor of pruning tolerance (CV spectrum: 0.038→0.11→0.151→0.215→0.314).\n3. A \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" framework with Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a ~22M parameter cross-attention module that recovers pixel-shuffle from 0% to 60.1% SR at 75% compression, and achieves 45-58% SR on MLP projectors at 75-87.5% compression.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- Benchmark: LIBERO manipulation benchmark, 500 episodes per configuration (50 per task × 10 tasks), Wilson 95% CI\n- Pruning: true token removal (not zero-masking) at k ∈ {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95, 1.0}\n- 6 models: π0 (3.3B, pure linear, 256 tokens, offline eval), RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler, closed-loop), X-VLA (880M, linear+BART 12L, 50 tokens, closed-loop), OpenVLA (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens, closed-loop), OpenVLA-OFT (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens, offline eval), SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle 16×, 128 tokens, closed-loop)\n- VTR: VTC-only (frozen LLM) and full VTR variants; M-sweep for SmolVLA (M=16,32,64,96,128) with 3-seed mean±std\n\n## Key Results Tables\nTable 2 (Taxonomy): CV spectrum 0.038→0.11→0.151→0.215→0.314 maps to immune→gentle→threshold→smooth→catastrophic\nTable 3 (OpenVLA pruning): k=1.0: 74%, k=0.95: 69.4%, k=0.9: 62%, k=0.7: 49%, k=0.5: 37.4%, k=0.3: 20.6%\nTable 4 (X-VLA): k=1.0: 99.2%, k=0.9: 97.6%, k=0.7: 56.2% (cliff), k=0.5: 42.6%, k=0.3: 23%\nTable 5 (RoboFlamingo): Baseline 42%, k=0.7: 28%, k=0.5: 15%, k=0.3: 4%\nTable 6 (SmolVLA VTR M-sweep): All M values within ±5pp, M=32: 60.1±1.3%, flat M-curve\nTable 7 (VTR universality): SmolVLA M=32: 60.1±1.3% vs 0% hard prune; OpenVLA M=32: 45% vs ~8%; M=64: 52% vs 20.6%; M=96: 58% vs 37.4%\nTable 8 (L2-norm vs random): max |Δ|=2.0pp across all k values\nTable 9 (Seed robustness): X-VLA k=0.7 seed42: 56.2% vs seed44: 52.0% (Δ=-4.2pp); OpenVLA k=0.7: 49% vs 47% (Δ=-2pp)\n\n## Imagined Experiments from Design\nThe experimental design includes 12 imagined experiments. The paper covers:\n- OpenVLA VTC-only M=32/64/96 (3 experiments) ✓\n- OpenVLA L2-norm pruning at k=0.95/0.9/0.7/0.5/0.3 (5 experiments) ✓\n- X-VLA seed44 replication at k=0.7 and k=0.9 (2 experiments) ✓\n- OpenVLA random pruning seed43 at k=0.7 and k=0.5 (2 experiments) ✓\n- RoboFlamingo Perceiver Resampler baseline + pruning (1 experiment) — partially covered (pruning curve shown but noted as bonus)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Methodology Assessment\n- Is the experimental methodology sound? Are the evaluation protocols appropriate?\n- Is the pruning protocol (true removal vs masking) well justified?\n- Is the CV diagnostic metric well-motivated and rigorously validated?\n- Is VTR's design (cross-attention re-encoding) well-justified against alternatives?\n- Are there methodological concerns (e.g., different eval modes for different models: offline for π0 and OFT, closed-loop for others)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are sample sizes sufficient (500 episodes per config)?\n- Is the seed robustness analysis adequate (only 2 seeds for a few configs)?\n- Are confidence intervals and statistical tests reported appropriately?\n- Are the VTR results convincing (3 seeds for SmolVLA, but single seed for OpenVLA VTC)?\n- Is the comparison fair across models with very different baselines (π0 99.2% vs RoboFlamingo 42%)?\n\n### 3. Completeness and Coverage\n- Does the paper cover the planned experiments adequately?\n- Are there missing ablations or experiments that would strengthen claims?\n- Is the RoboFlamingo experiment convincingly presented?\n\n### 4. Specific Concerns\n- π0 is evaluated offline (normalized L1 loss) not closed-loop. Is this a significant limitation?\n- RoboFlamingo baseline is 42% — does this low baseline limit interpretability?\n- The paper claims VTR is \"universal\" but only tests on 2 connector types\n- VTR adds 22M parameters and requires fine-tuning — how does this compare to parameter-free alternatives?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)  \n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Methods Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Experiments Score** (1-10, with justification)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"777d3eaf-5a72-4767-9088-6ce6d3df81c6","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:37:51.258Z"}
