{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C6Wnx4XF3BJR9Wf3Asmwbn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\n14\t\\definecolor{immune}{RGB}{34,139,34}\n15\t\\definecolor{graded}{RGB}{255,165,0}\n16\t\\definecolor{catastrophic}{RGB}{220,20,60}\n17\t\\definecolor{threshold}{RGB}{70,130,180}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others:\\\\The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility}\n20\t\n21\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n22\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t%% ============================================================\n30\t%% ABSTRACT\n31\t%% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models process hundreds of visual tokens per frame, creating a bottleneck for real-time robotic deployment. While inference-time token reduction methods have shown promise, their effectiveness varies dramatically across architectures for reasons that remain unexplained. We present the first systematic study of token pruning robustness across VLA architectures, testing 6 models spanning 4 connector families and model sizes from 450M to 7B parameters. We discover that the \\emph{effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline}---not model size---determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum. Pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) is pruning-immune (loss \\emph{improves} 2.4\\% at 90\\% token removal). Linear projection followed by deep post-processing (X-VLA, 12-layer BART encoder) exhibits a threshold-graded pattern: robust at $k \\geq 0.9$ (97.6\\% SR) but cliffs at $k=0.7$ (56.2\\%). Learnable cross-attention queries (RoboFlamingo, Perceiver Resampler) show gentle graded degradation. Multi-layer MLP connectors (OpenVLA) degrade smoothly ($k=0.5 \\to 37.4\\%$ SR). Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) collapse catastrophically---removing just 5\\% of tokens yields 0\\% success rate. The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance scores predicts this spectrum: $\\text{CV} = 0.038$ (immune) $\\to 0.11$ (gentle) $\\to 0.151$ (threshold) $\\to 0.215$ (smooth) $\\to 0.314$ (catastrophic). Based on this analysis, we propose a Diagnose-then-Compress framework: (1) a zero-cost CV diagnostic that predicts pruning tolerance from a single forward pass, and (2) Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M parameters) that provides universal token compression across connector types---recovering pixel-shuffle from 0\\% to 60.1\\% SR at 75\\% compression, and achieving 45--58\\% SR on MLP projectors at 75--87.5\\% compression where hard pruning retains only 8--21\\%.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t%% ============================================================\n37\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t%% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as a promising paradigm for generalist robot policies, unifying vision understanding and action generation in a single transformer architecture~\\cite{brohan2023rt2,kim2024openvla,black2024pi0}. However, VLAs inherit a critical efficiency bottleneck from their VLM backbones: the vision encoder produces hundreds of tokens per frame (e.g., 256 for OpenVLA, 128 for SmolVLA), which dominate the LLM's key-value cache and attention computation during autoregressive action decoding.\n42\t\n43\tA growing literature proposes inference-time token reduction to address this bottleneck. Methods such as FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, and ToMe have demonstrated that significant portions of visual tokens can be removed with minimal performance loss on certain architectures. However, these results are architecture-specific: each method is typically evaluated on a single VLA, and the implicit assumption that token pruning generalizes across architectures remains untested.\n44\t\n45\tWe challenge this assumption through the first systematic cross-architecture study of VLA token compressibility. Testing 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families and sizes from 450M to 7B parameters on the LIBERO manipulation benchmark, we discover that pruning tolerance is determined by the \\emph{effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline}---the complete pathway from vision encoder output to LLM input, including any intermediate processing layers.\n46\t\n47\tOur key findings reveal a clean four-class taxonomy:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Immune}: Pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) can tolerate 90\\% token removal with loss actually \\emph{improving} by 2.4\\%, because linearly projected tokens carry independent, uniformly distributed information (CV${}=0.038$).\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Threshold-graded}: Linear projection followed by deep post-processing (X-VLA, 12-layer BART encoder) is robust at $k \\geq 0.9$ (97.6\\% SR) but exhibits a cliff at $k=0.7$ (56.2\\% SR, $-43$pp). The BART encoder introduces partial token dependencies that create a pruning threshold.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Smooth graded}: Multi-layer MLP connectors (OpenVLA, 3-layer MLP with GELU) degrade monotonically ($k=0.5 \\to 37.4\\%$ SR, $-49\\%$ relative). Non-linear mixing creates partial dependencies, but tokens remain individually meaningful.\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Catastrophic}: Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA, $16\\times$ spatial folding) collapse at even 5\\% removal ($k=0.95 \\to 0\\%$ SR). Spatial folding creates irreversible cross-token entanglement.\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\tThe coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance scores serves as a zero-cost continuous predictor of pruning tolerance: lower CV indicates later onset of degradation. Critically, model size is \\emph{not} the explanatory variable---$\\pi_0$ (3.3B, linear) is immune while OpenVLA (7B, MLP) degrades significantly, and X-VLA (880M) shows intermediate behavior matching its intermediate CV.\n56\t\n57\tBased on this diagnostic analysis, we propose Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M parameters) that learns to re-encode visual tokens into a compressed representation. On SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector, VTR recovers from 0\\% (hard pruning) to 60.1$\\pm$1.3\\% SR at 75\\% compression, with the M-curve confirmed flat across all compression ratios---demonstrating near-lossless compression for entangled tokens. We further show that VTR provides \\emph{universal} compression across connector types: on OpenVLA's MLP projector, VTC-only achieves 45\\% SR at 87.5\\% compression (M=32) and 52\\% SR at 75\\% compression (M=64), substantially outperforming hard pruning at equivalent compression rates.\n58\t\n59\t%% ============================================================\n60\t%% 2. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n61\t%% ============================================================\n62\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{Vision-Language-Action Models}\n65\t\n66\tVLA models cast robotic manipulation as conditional text generation: given a language instruction and visual observation, an LLM backbone autoregressively generates discretized actions. The \\emph{vision connector} bridges the vision encoder (typically a frozen SigLIP or DINOv2) and the LLM by projecting image features into the LLM's token embedding space. This connector varies significantly across architectures:\n67\t\n68\t\\begin{itemize}\n69\t    \\item \\textbf{Pure linear projection} ($\\pi_0$): A single linear layer maps each vision patch to an LLM token. Tokens retain the spatial independence of the vision encoder's output.\n70\t    \\item \\textbf{Linear projection + deep post-processing} (X-VLA): Linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder that introduces cross-token mixing through self-attention.\n71\t    \\item \\textbf{Perceiver Resampler} (RoboFlamingo): Learnable cross-attention queries attend to vision features, producing a fixed set of aggregated tokens.\n72\t    \\item \\textbf{Multi-layer MLP} (OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT): A 3-layer MLP with GELU activations independently processes each token but introduces non-linear mixing within each token's representation.\n73\t    \\item \\textbf{Pixel-shuffle} (SmolVLA): $16\\times$ spatial folding rearranges 1024 patches into 64 tokens per camera by folding spatial dimensions into channels, creating extreme cross-token entanglement.\n74\t\\end{itemize}\n75\t\n76\t\\subsection{Token Reduction for Vision-Language Models}\n77\t\n78\tInference-time token reduction methods fall into two categories. \\emph{Token pruning} methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O) remove tokens based on importance scores, typically derived from attention weights. \\emph{Token merging} methods (ToMe) combine similar tokens. Recent concurrent works---Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, and ``See What Matters''---all propose pruning specifically on MLP-projector VLAs. Our work differs by (1) explaining \\emph{why} pruning fails on pixel-shuffle architectures, (2) identifying \\emph{why} pruning succeeds on linear projections, and (3) proposing a diagnostic-guided re-encoding method motivated by cross-architecture analysis.\n79\t\n80\t%% ============================================================\n81\t%% 3. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n82\t%% ============================================================\n83\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Models}\n86\t\n87\tWe evaluate 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families and sizes from 450M to 7B parameters (Table~\\ref{tab:models}).\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{table}[h]\n90\t\\centering\n91\t\\caption{VLA models evaluated, ordered by connector complexity.}\n92\t\\label{tab:models}\n93\t\\small\n94\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n95\t\\toprule\n96\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Connector} & \\textbf{Size} & \\textbf{Tokens} & \\textbf{Eval Mode} \\\\\n97\t\\midrule\n98\t$\\pi_0$ & Pure linear projection & 3.3B & 256 & Offline (loss) \\\\\n99\tRoboFlamingo & Perceiver Resampler & --- & --- & Closed-loop \\\\\n100\tX-VLA & Linear + BART 12L & 880M & 50 & Closed-loop \\\\\n101\tOpenVLA & 3-layer MLP (GELU) & 7B & 256 & Closed-loop \\\\\n102\tOpenVLA-OFT & 3-layer MLP (GELU) & 7B & 256 & Offline (entropy) \\\\\n103\tSmolVLA & Pixel-shuffle $16\\times$ & 450M & 128 & Closed-loop \\\\\n104\t\\bottomrule\n105\t\\end{tabular}\n106\t\\end{table}\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Benchmark and Protocol}\n109\t\n110\tClosed-loop evaluations use the LIBERO manipulation benchmark, evaluating 500 episodes per configuration across 10 tasks (50 per task). We report overall success rate (SR) with Wilson 95\\% confidence intervals. Pruning is implemented as true token removal (not zero-masking) at retention ratio $k \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95, 1.0\\}$. For $\\pi_0$, we use offline normalized L1 loss as the evaluation metric due to its flow-matching action head. Seed robustness is verified by replicating key configurations with alternative random seeds.\n111\t\n112\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n113\t\n114\tWe compute two zero-cost diagnostic metrics from a single forward pass:\n115\t\\begin{itemize}\n116\t    \\item \\textbf{Importance CV}: Coefficient of variation of L2-norm token importance scores across visual tokens. Higher CV indicates more heterogeneous importance distribution.\n117\t    \\item \\textbf{Attention entropy ratio}: Mean entropy of suffix-to-vision attention weights, normalized by maximum entropy. Values near 1.0 indicate uniform attention; lower values indicate selective attention.\n118\t\\end{itemize}\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR)}\n121\t\n122\tVTR is a lightweight cross-attention module inserted between the vision connector and the LLM. $M$ learnable queries attend to the $N$ connector output tokens via cross-attention, producing $M$ re-encoded tokens ($M \\ll N$). The module has $\\sim$22M parameters and is trained with the LLM backbone frozen (VTC-only) or jointly (full VTR). We evaluate VTC-only on both SmolVLA and OpenVLA connectors.\n123\t\n124\t%% ============================================================\n125\t%% 4. MAIN RESULTS\n126\t%% ============================================================\n127\t\\section{Main Results}\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Six-Model Taxonomy}\n130\t\n131\tTable~\\ref{tab:taxonomy} presents the complete taxonomy of pruning behaviors across 6 models and 4 connector families. The CV of token importance scores forms a continuous spectrum that predicts pruning class.\n132\t\n133\t\\begin{table}[h]\n134\t\\centering\n135\t\\caption{Extended 6-model taxonomy of VLA token pruning behavior. CV forms a continuous predictor of pruning tolerance. $k{=}0.5$ SR serves as a discriminative reference point.}\n136\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n137\t\\small\n138\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n139\t\\toprule\n140\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Connector} & \\textbf{CV} & \\textbf{Entropy} & \\textbf{Pruning Class} & \\textbf{$k{=}0.5$ SR} \\\\\n141\t\\midrule\n142\t$\\pi_0$ (3.3B) & Pure linear & 0.038 & 0.959 & Immune & N/A (offline) \\\\\n143\tRoboFlamingo & Perceiver Resampler & 0.11 & 0.88 & Gentle graded & 15\\% \\\\\n144\tX-VLA (880M) & Linear + BART 12L & 0.151 & 0.614 & Threshold-graded & 42.6\\% \\\\\n145\tOpenVLA (7B) & 3-layer MLP & 0.215 & 0.661 & Smooth graded & 37.4\\% \\\\\n146\tOFT (7B) & 3-layer MLP & --- & 0.667 & ($\\approx$ OpenVLA) & --- \\\\\n147\tSmolVLA (450M) & Pixel-shuffle $16\\times$ & 0.314 & 0.952 & Catastrophic & 0\\% \\\\\n148\t\\bottomrule\n149\t\\end{tabular}\n150\t\\end{table}\n151\t\n152\tThe taxonomy reveals several critical observations. First, model size does not predict pruning tolerance: $\\pi_0$ (3.3B) is immune while OpenVLA (7B) degrades substantially, and the smallest model (SmolVLA, 450M) exhibits the most extreme failure mode. Second, the CV spectrum ($0.038 \\to 0.11 \\to 0.151 \\to 0.215 \\to 0.314$) monotonically predicts degradation onset, functioning as a continuous predictor rather than a discrete classifier. Third, OpenVLA-OFT's attention entropy (0.667) closely matches standard OpenVLA (0.661), confirming that the vision connector---not the action head---determines attention structure and pruning behavior.\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Catastrophic: Pixel-Shuffle Cliff-Edge (SmolVLA)}\n155\t\n156\tSmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector folds 1024 SigLIP patches into 64 tokens per camera via $16\\times$ spatial compression. This creates extreme cross-token entanglement: each token encodes a $32\\times32$ pixel region through channel-to-spatial reorganization, leaving no redundancy between tokens.\n157\t\n158\tThe result is a catastrophic cliff-edge: removing even 5\\% of tokens ($k=0.95$, 7 of 128 tokens) collapses performance to 0\\% SR from a 44.8\\% baseline. This holds across all pruning strategies tested---random removal, ACIS (attention-based), ToMe (merging), and geometric priors (Sobel edge detection)---confirming that the failure is architectural, not method-specific. Official checkpoint evaluation on both Object ($59.2\\% \\to 0\\%$) and Spatial ($43.8\\% \\to 0.6\\%$, 3/500 episodes) suites confirms the cliff-edge generalizes across task distributions. The attention entropy ratio of 0.952 (near-uniform, total variation 0.05) indicates that the LLM attends to all visual tokens with near-equal weight, consistent with each token carrying irreplaceable spatial information.\n159\t\n160\t\\subsection{Smooth Graded: MLP Degradation (OpenVLA)}\n161\t\n162\tOpenVLA's 3-layer MLP connector with GELU activations processes each vision patch independently but introduces non-linear mixing within representations. This creates partial token dependencies while preserving individual token meaningfulness.\n163\t\n164\t\\begin{table}[h]\n165\t\\centering\n166\t\\caption{OpenVLA pruning degradation curve. Baseline $k{=}1.0$: 74.0\\% SR (370/500). All results averaged over 500 episodes.}\n167\t\\label{tab:openvla_curve}\n168\t\\small\n169\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n170\t\\toprule\n171\t$k$ & \\textbf{Tokens Kept} & \\textbf{SR (\\%)} & \\textbf{Relative $\\Delta$} \\\\\n172\t\\midrule\n173\t1.00 & 256 & 74.0 & --- \\\\\n174\t0.95 & 243 & 69.4 & $-6\\%$ \\\\\n175\t0.90 & 230 & 62.0 & $-16\\%$ \\\\\n176\t0.70 & 179 & 49.0 & $-34\\%$ \\\\\n177\t0.50 & 128 & 37.4 & $-49\\%$ \\\\\n178\t0.30 & 77 & 20.6 & $-72\\%$ \\\\\n179\t\\bottomrule\n180\t\\end{tabular}\n181\t\\end{table}\n182\t\n183\tTable~\\ref{tab:openvla_curve} shows smooth, monotonic degradation with no cliff---a stark contrast to SmolVLA's catastrophic collapse. Even at $k=0.3$ (70\\% token removal), OpenVLA maintains 20.6\\% SR. The attention entropy of 0.661 (progressive sharpening from 0.98 at layer 0 to 0.48 at layer 29, total variation 0.50) indicates increasingly selective attention through the LLM, consistent with partial but non-catastrophic token dependencies.\n184\t\n185\t\\subsection{Threshold-Graded: Linear + Deep Post-Processing (X-VLA)}\n186\t\n187\tX-VLA uses a linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder, introducing substantial cross-token mixing through self-attention. This creates a distinctive threshold-graded pattern: robustness above the threshold and graded degradation below.\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{X-VLA pruning curve showing threshold-graded pattern. Baseline $k{=}1.0$: 99.2\\% SR. Per-task SR shows extreme variance at the cliff point ($k{=}0.7$).}\n192\t\\label{tab:xvla_curve}\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t$k$ & \\textbf{SR (\\%)} & $\\Delta$ \\textbf{(pp)} & \\textbf{Pattern} \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\t1.00 & 99.2 & --- & \\textcolor{immune}{Baseline} \\\\\n199\t0.95 & 99.0 & $-0.2$ & \\textcolor{immune}{Immune} \\\\\n200\t0.90 & 97.6 & $-1.6$ & \\textcolor{immune}{Immune} \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"e760c063-5946-4133-9b5f-f90dfa4ddb88","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:14.930Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019sGVVcUsysCrcgGUPVfH6r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RHtEauE9hZuq6K1UhjzGj7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/_project/paper/results_draft.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":659,"cache_read_input_tokens":41626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":659,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":66,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"54453d11-b324-427c-8471-b6e6dee3a323"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RHtEauE9hZuq6K1UhjzGj7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\tWe evaluate token pruning across two VLA architectures on the LIBERO-Object benchmark~\\cite{liu2023libero}, comprising 10 tasks with 50 episodes each (500 total). All success rates are reported with Wilson score 95\\% confidence intervals.\n5\t\n6\t\\subsection{SmolVLA: Cliff-Edge Under All Pruning Methods}\n7\t\\label{sec:smolvla_results}\n8\t\n9\tSmolVLA~\\cite{shukor2025smolvla} uses a pixel-shuffle vision connector that spatially rearranges patch embeddings before projecting them into the language model's embedding space. This operation creates inter-token spatial dependencies: each output token encodes information from a local grid of input patches. We hypothesize that this coupling makes the token sequence fragile under any form of pruning, since removing or masking a single token disrupts the spatial structure that downstream layers expect.\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n12\tWe use a SmolVLA checkpoint fine-tuned on LIBERO-Object with the ACIS (Adaptive Contextual Importance Scoring) module attached but pruning disabled during training. The baseline (bypass mode) achieves 44.8\\% SR (224/500, CI [40.5\\%, 49.2\\%]). We then activate pruning under six configurations that vary three axes: scoring function (learned ACIS vs.\\ random), retention ratio $k \\in \\{0.5, 0.7, 0.9\\}$, and pruning mechanism (zero-masking, true token removal, token merging via ToMe~\\cite{bolya2023tome}).\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{All configurations collapse to 0\\% SR.}\n15\tTable~\\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation} reports the full ablation. Every pruning configuration produces 0\\% success rate across all 500 episodes (Wilson CI [0\\%, 0.76\\%]). Three observations make this result striking:\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{enumerate}\n18\t    \\item \\textbf{Scoring-independent.} Random token selection performs identically to the learned ACIS scorer. The failure does not stem from poor importance estimation.\n19\t    \\item \\textbf{Ratio-independent.} Retaining 90\\% of tokens ($k{=}0.9$, removing only 10\\%) causes the same catastrophic failure as retaining 50\\%. There is no graceful degradation at any operating point.\n20\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanism-independent.} Zero-masking, true removal (sequence shortening), and token merging all produce identical 0\\% SR. The failure is not an artifact of a particular implementation choice.\n21\t\\end{enumerate}\n22\t\n23\tThe convergence of all six conditions to 0\\% SR, against a 44.8\\% baseline, constitutes a cliff-edge failure pattern: performance does not degrade gradually but collapses entirely once any tokens are disturbed.\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{table}[t]\n26\t\\centering\n27\t\\small\n28\t\\caption{SmolVLA token pruning ablation on LIBERO-Object. All six pruning configurations collapse to 0\\% SR regardless of scoring function, retention ratio, or mechanism. The baseline achieves 44.8\\% with pruning disabled.}\n29\t\\label{tab:smolvla_ablation}\n30\t\\begin{tabular}{llccrr}\n31\t\\toprule\n32\tScoring & $k$ & Mechanism & SR (\\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\\% CI \\\\\n33\t\\midrule\n34\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Baseline (bypass)}} & -- & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\\\\n35\t\\midrule\n36\tACIS & 0.5 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n37\tACIS & 0.7 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n38\tACIS & 0.9 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n39\tRandom & 0.5 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n40\tACIS & 0.5 & True removal & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.80] \\\\\n41\tACIS & 0.5 & Merge (ToMe) & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\\\\n42\t\\bottomrule\n43\t\\end{tabular}\n44\t\\end{table}\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Geometric prior confirms the pattern.}\n47\tAs additional evidence, we evaluate a non-learned geometric prior (Sobel edge-based scoring) at $k \\in \\{0.5, 0.7, 0.9\\}$, all producing 0\\% SR.\\footnote{The geometric prior experiments use a different model checkpoint than the ACIS bypass baseline, so we report them separately from Table~\\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}. The qualitative conclusion is identical: any form of token disturbance causes complete failure.}\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Cross-suite confirmation.}\n50\tThe cliff-edge pattern generalizes beyond LIBERO-Object. On LIBERO-Spatial (baseline 51.8\\% SR) and LIBERO-Goal (baseline 63.0\\% SR), ACIS pruning at $k{=}0.5$ with true removal produces 0\\% SR in both suites (0/500 episodes each, CI [0\\%, 0.76\\%]). The failure is not task-specific but architectural.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Interpretation.}\n53\tThe pixel-shuffle connector in SmolVLA creates a structured token lattice where spatial adjacency carries semantic meaning. When any subset of tokens is removed, masked, or merged, the resulting sequence violates the implicit spatial contract that downstream transformer layers learned during pretraining. This interpretation aligns with the connector's design: pixel-shuffle compresses spatial resolution by interleaving channels, producing tokens whose meaning depends on their position within the original grid. We formalize this analysis in Section~\\ref{sec:analysis}.\n54\t\n55\t\n56\t\\subsection{OpenVLA: Graceful Degradation}\n57\t\\label{sec:openvla_results}\n58\t\n59\tOpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} uses a two-layer MLP projector to map vision encoder outputs (from DINOv2 and SigLIP~\\cite{zhai2023siglip}) into the Llama-2 language model's embedding space. Unlike pixel-shuffle, the MLP projector processes each patch token independently through a shared linear transformation. This design preserves per-token independence: the representation of one token carries no implicit dependency on neighboring tokens' presence.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n62\tWe evaluate the OpenVLA-7B checkpoint on LIBERO-Object under random token removal (true removal, no learned scorer). The baseline achieves 74.0\\% SR (370/500 episodes). We apply pruning at $k{=}0.5$, retaining 50\\% of vision tokens selected uniformly at random.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Graded degradation instead of collapse.}\n65\tTable~\\ref{tab:openvla_degradation} shows the results. At $k{=}0.5$, OpenVLA achieves 37.4\\% SR (187/500), a relative drop of 49.5\\% from baseline. This stands in sharp contrast to SmolVLA, where the same 50\\% retention ratio produces 0\\% SR. OpenVLA loses performance proportionally to the information removed, rather than collapsing catastrophically.\n66\t\n67\t\\begin{table}[t]\n68\t\\centering\n69\t\\small\n70\t\\caption{OpenVLA token pruning on LIBERO-Object. Unlike SmolVLA, OpenVLA shows graded degradation under random token removal, consistent with its MLP projector preserving per-token independence.}\n71\t\\label{tab:openvla_degradation}\n72\t\\begin{tabular}{cccr}\n73\t\\toprule\n74\t$k$ & SR (\\%) & Relative Drop (\\%) & Episodes \\\\\n75\t\\midrule\n76\t1.0 (baseline) & 74.0 & -- & 500 \\\\\n77\t0.5 & 37.4 & 49.5 & 500 \\\\\n78\t\\bottomrule\n79\t\\end{tabular}\n80\t\\end{table}\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Per-task analysis reveals graded variance.}\n83\tThe per-task breakdown at $k{=}0.5$ shows a wide range of success rates: from 2\\% (butter) to 72\\% (orange\\_juice). Tasks involving visually distinct target objects (orange\\_juice, alphabet\\_soup at 60\\%) retain higher success rates, while tasks requiring fine-grained visual discrimination among similar objects (butter at 2\\%, cream\\_cheese at 8\\%) suffer the most. This graded, task-dependent degradation pattern confirms that OpenVLA retains partial visual information under pruning rather than losing all task-relevant signal.\n84\t\n85\t\\paragraph{Architectural contrast.}\n86\tThe divergence between SmolVLA and OpenVLA under identical pruning conditions (random selection, $k{=}0.5$, true removal) isolates the vision connector as the determining factor. Both models receive the same visual input and operate on the same benchmark. The only structural difference in the vision-to-language pathway is the connector: pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA) versus MLP projector (OpenVLA). SmolVLA drops from 44.8\\% to 0\\%; OpenVLA drops from 74.0\\% to 37.4\\%. This architecture-dependent compressibility gap motivates our analysis of token spatial structure in Section~\\ref{sec:analysis}.\n87\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"78022785-d745-457a-90ef-e42af6534a0e","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:15.492Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01A5ZipPSgG9d4n2kjvfrCcG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DE8KKbTR1R3Uc24fbjeCTk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents the first systematic cross-architecture study of visual token pruning robustness across 6 VLA models (π0, RoboFlamingo, X-VLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, SmolVLA) spanning 4 connector families (pure linear, Perceiver Resampler, linear+BART, MLP, pixel-shuffle). Key contributions:\n1. Discovery that the vision-to-LLM projection pipeline — not model size — determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum\n2. A four-class taxonomy: immune (CV=0.038), gentle graded (CV=0.11), threshold-graded (CV=0.151), smooth graded (CV=0.215), catastrophic (CV=0.314)\n3. Importance CV as a zero-cost continuous predictor\n4. Diagnose-then-Compress framework with VTR (~22M params cross-attention module)\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper\nSection 2.1 covers VLA models: RT-2 [1], OpenVLA [2], π0 [3], SmolVLA [4], X-VLA [5]\nSection 2.2 covers token reduction: FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, ToMe (token merging). Concurrent works: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, \"See What Matters\" — all propose pruning for MLP-projector VLAs.\n\nThe paper claims three differentiators: (1) explaining why pruning fails on pixel-shuffle, (2) identifying why pruning succeeds on linear projections, (3) proposing diagnostic-guided re-encoding motivated by cross-architecture analysis.\n\n## References (10 total)\n[1] Brohan et al. RT-2, CoRL 2023\n[2] Kim et al. OpenVLA, CoRL 2024\n[3] Black et al. π0, arXiv 2024\n[4] Zhu et al. SmolVLA, arXiv 2025\n[5] X-VLA Team. X-VLA, arXiv 2025\n[6] Li et al. VLMs as robot imitators, ICLR 2024\n[7] Liu et al. LIBERO, NeurIPS 2023\n[8] Chen et al. \"An image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2\", ECCV 2024\n[9] Zhang et al. H2O, NeurIPS 2023\n[10] Bolya et al. ToMe, ICLR 2023\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the cross-architecture pruning study? Has anyone done systematic comparisons before?\n- Is the CV-as-predictor finding genuinely novel or somewhat expected (higher CV → more heterogeneous → more vulnerable)?\n- Is the four-class taxonomy a genuine intellectual contribution or just a description of observed data?\n- Is VTR novel as a method, or is cross-attention re-encoding well-known in other contexts (e.g., Perceiver, Q-Former)?\n- How does this compare to concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters)?\n\n### 2. Significance for CoRL Community\n- Is this relevant to the robot learning community specifically, or is it more of a VLM efficiency paper?\n- Does the paper provide actionable guidance for practitioners deploying VLAs?\n- Would reviewers at CoRL value the diagnostic (CV) tool?\n- Is the LIBERO-only evaluation sufficient for a robotics venue, or would real-robot experiments be expected?\n\n### 3. Related Work Completeness\n- Only 10 references — is this sufficient for a CoRL submission?\n- Are important related works missing? Consider: Perceiver/Perceiver IO, Q-Former/BLIP-2, FlexAttention, visual token compression in VLMs more broadly, other VLA architectures (Octo, RT-H, etc.)\n- The concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) are mentioned but not compared experimentally. Is this a gap?\n- Is the positioning relative to token merging methods (ToMe) adequate? Only mentioned briefly.\n\n### 4. Writing and Presentation\n- Is the narrative structure (diagnosis → taxonomy → method) effective?\n- Are claims well-calibrated or overclaimed?\n- The title is attention-grabbing — does the content deliver?\n\n### 5. Specific Concerns\n- The paper claims \"first systematic cross-architecture study\" — verify if this is actually true\n- The CV predictor is validated on only 5-6 data points (one per model). Is this sufficient to claim it's a \"continuous predictor\"?\n- VTR is called \"universal\" but only tested on 2 of 4 connector types\n- No real-robot experiments — how critical is this for CoRL?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Novelty Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Significance Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Related Work Score** (1-10, with justification)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1911,"cache_read_input_tokens":50482,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1911},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"9da75569-ad88-4032-8a4b-9718718c54bf"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aa1d2550616220eb3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01DE8KKbTR1R3Uc24fbjeCTk","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents the first systematic cross-architecture study of visual token pruning robustness across 6 VLA models (π0, RoboFlamingo, X-VLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, SmolVLA) spanning 4 connector families (pure linear, Perceiver Resampler, linear+BART, MLP, pixel-shuffle). Key contributions:\n1. Discovery that the vision-to-LLM projection pipeline — not model size — determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum\n2. A four-class taxonomy: immune (CV=0.038), gentle graded (CV=0.11), threshold-graded (CV=0.151), smooth graded (CV=0.215), catastrophic (CV=0.314)\n3. Importance CV as a zero-cost continuous predictor\n4. Diagnose-then-Compress framework with VTR (~22M params cross-attention module)\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper\nSection 2.1 covers VLA models: RT-2 [1], OpenVLA [2], π0 [3], SmolVLA [4], X-VLA [5]\nSection 2.2 covers token reduction: FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, ToMe (token merging). Concurrent works: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, \"See What Matters\" — all propose pruning for MLP-projector VLAs.\n\nThe paper claims three differentiators: (1) explaining why pruning fails on pixel-shuffle, (2) identifying why pruning succeeds on linear projections, (3) proposing diagnostic-guided re-encoding motivated by cross-architecture analysis.\n\n## References (10 total)\n[1] Brohan et al. RT-2, CoRL 2023\n[2] Kim et al. OpenVLA, CoRL 2024\n[3] Black et al. π0, arXiv 2024\n[4] Zhu et al. SmolVLA, arXiv 2025\n[5] X-VLA Team. X-VLA, arXiv 2025\n[6] Li et al. VLMs as robot imitators, ICLR 2024\n[7] Liu et al. LIBERO, NeurIPS 2023\n[8] Chen et al. \"An image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2\", ECCV 2024\n[9] Zhang et al. H2O, NeurIPS 2023\n[10] Bolya et al. ToMe, ICLR 2023\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the cross-architecture pruning study? Has anyone done systematic comparisons before?\n- Is the CV-as-predictor finding genuinely novel or somewhat expected (higher CV → more heterogeneous → more vulnerable)?\n- Is the four-class taxonomy a genuine intellectual contribution or just a description of observed data?\n- Is VTR novel as a method, or is cross-attention re-encoding well-known in other contexts (e.g., Perceiver, Q-Former)?\n- How does this compare to concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters)?\n\n### 2. Significance for CoRL Community\n- Is this relevant to the robot learning community specifically, or is it more of a VLM efficiency paper?\n- Does the paper provide actionable guidance for practitioners deploying VLAs?\n- Would reviewers at CoRL value the diagnostic (CV) tool?\n- Is the LIBERO-only evaluation sufficient for a robotics venue, or would real-robot experiments be expected?\n\n### 3. Related Work Completeness\n- Only 10 references — is this sufficient for a CoRL submission?\n- Are important related works missing? Consider: Perceiver/Perceiver IO, Q-Former/BLIP-2, FlexAttention, visual token compression in VLMs more broadly, other VLA architectures (Octo, RT-H, etc.)\n- The concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) are mentioned but not compared experimentally. Is this a gap?\n- Is the positioning relative to token merging methods (ToMe) adequate? Only mentioned briefly.\n\n### 4. Writing and Presentation\n- Is the narrative structure (diagnosis → taxonomy → method) effective?\n- Are claims well-calibrated or overclaimed?\n- The title is attention-grabbing — does the content deliver?\n\n### 5. Specific Concerns\n- The paper claims \"first systematic cross-architecture study\" — verify if this is actually true\n- The CV predictor is validated on only 5-6 data points (one per model). Is this sufficient to claim it's a \"continuous predictor\"?\n- VTR is called \"universal\" but only tested on 2 of 4 connector types\n- No real-robot experiments — how critical is this for CoRL?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Novelty Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Significance Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Related Work Score** (1-10, with justification)","uuid":"09ae8b46-dffa-41f7-a008-acde7c78e94f","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents the first systematic cross-architecture study of visual token pruning robustness across 6 VLA models (π0, RoboFlamingo, X-VLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, SmolVLA) spanning 4 connector families (pure linear, Perceiver Resampler, linear+BART, MLP, pixel-shuffle). Key contributions:\n1. Discovery that the vision-to-LLM projection pipeline — not model size — determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum\n2. A four-class taxonomy: immune (CV=0.038), gentle graded (CV=0.11), threshold-graded (CV=0.151), smooth graded (CV=0.215), catastrophic (CV=0.314)\n3. Importance CV as a zero-cost continuous predictor\n4. Diagnose-then-Compress framework with VTR (~22M params cross-attention module)\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper\nSection 2.1 covers VLA models: RT-2 [1], OpenVLA [2], π0 [3], SmolVLA [4], X-VLA [5]\nSection 2.2 covers token reduction: FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, ToMe (token merging). Concurrent works: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, \"See What Matters\" — all propose pruning for MLP-projector VLAs.\n\nThe paper claims three differentiators: (1) explaining why pruning fails on pixel-shuffle, (2) identifying why pruning succeeds on linear projections, (3) proposing diagnostic-guided re-encoding motivated by cross-architecture analysis.\n\n## References (10 total)\n[1] Brohan et al. RT-2, CoRL 2023\n[2] Kim et al. OpenVLA, CoRL 2024\n[3] Black et al. π0, arXiv 2024\n[4] Zhu et al. SmolVLA, arXiv 2025\n[5] X-VLA Team. X-VLA, arXiv 2025\n[6] Li et al. VLMs as robot imitators, ICLR 2024\n[7] Liu et al. LIBERO, NeurIPS 2023\n[8] Chen et al. \"An image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2\", ECCV 2024\n[9] Zhang et al. H2O, NeurIPS 2023\n[10] Bolya et al. ToMe, ICLR 2023\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the cross-architecture pruning study? Has anyone done systematic comparisons before?\n- Is the CV-as-predictor finding genuinely novel or somewhat expected (higher CV → more heterogeneous → more vulnerable)?\n- Is the four-class taxonomy a genuine intellectual contribution or just a description of observed data?\n- Is VTR novel as a method, or is cross-attention re-encoding well-known in other contexts (e.g., Perceiver, Q-Former)?\n- How does this compare to concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters)?\n\n### 2. Significance for CoRL Community\n- Is this relevant to the robot learning community specifically, or is it more of a VLM efficiency paper?\n- Does the paper provide actionable guidance for practitioners deploying VLAs?\n- Would reviewers at CoRL value the diagnostic (CV) tool?\n- Is the LIBERO-only evaluation sufficient for a robotics venue, or would real-robot experiments be expected?\n\n### 3. Related Work Completeness\n- Only 10 references — is this sufficient for a CoRL submission?\n- Are important related works missing? Consider: Perceiver/Perceiver IO, Q-Former/BLIP-2, FlexAttention, visual token compression in VLMs more broadly, other VLA architectures (Octo, RT-H, etc.)\n- The concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) are mentioned but not compared experimentally. Is this a gap?\n- Is the positioning relative to token merging methods (ToMe) adequate? Only mentioned briefly.\n\n### 4. Writing and Presentation\n- Is the narrative structure (diagnosis → taxonomy → method) effective?\n- Are claims well-calibrated or overclaimed?\n- The title is attention-grabbing — does the content deliver?\n\n### 5. Specific Concerns\n- The paper claims \"first systematic cross-architecture study\" — verify if this is actually true\n- The CV predictor is validated on only 5-6 data points (one per model). Is this sufficient to claim it's a \"continuous predictor\"?\n- VTR is called \"universal\" but only tested on 2 of 4 connector types\n- No real-robot experiments — how critical is this for CoRL?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Novelty Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Significance Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Related Work Score** (1-10, with justification)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DE8KKbTR1R3Uc24fbjeCTk","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"3494fda8-3d7a-4d1f-a0b2-65f325955c79","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:17.240Z"}
