{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013Hny5iVmm5YvZc1AiZ4cf8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R1oe2CaYiaBfaaAVk57uPT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T155932/best/paper.tex","offset":200,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8198,"cache_read_input_tokens":42285,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8198,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"d61b0f91-5a58-4471-91a8-46a7533542dd"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R1oe2CaYiaBfaaAVk57uPT","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t0.90 & 97.6 & $-1.6$ & \\textcolor{immune}{Immune} \\\\\n201\t0.70 & 56.2 & $-43.0$ & \\textcolor{catastrophic}{Cliff} \\\\\n202\t0.50 & 42.6 & $-56.6$ & \\textcolor{graded}{Graded} \\\\\n203\t0.30 & 23.0 & $-76.2$ & \\textcolor{graded}{Graded} \\\\\n204\t0.20 & $\\sim$4.0 & $-95.2$ & \\textcolor{graded}{Near-zero} \\\\\n205\t0.10 & $\\sim$0.0 & $-99.2$ & \\textcolor{catastrophic}{Failure} \\\\\n206\t\\bottomrule\n207\t\\end{tabular}\n208\t\\end{table}\n209\t\n210\tThe threshold at $k=0.7$ marks the point where BART encoder's learned token dependencies become critical---removing 30\\% of tokens disrupts information pathways that the encoder has optimized during pretraining. Below this threshold, degradation is graded rather than catastrophic, distinguishing X-VLA from SmolVLA's all-or-nothing cliff. This intermediate pattern is correctly predicted by X-VLA's intermediate CV (0.151), positioned between $\\pi_0$'s 0.038 and OpenVLA's 0.215.\n211\t\n212\t\\subsection{Gentle Graded: Perceiver Resampler (RoboFlamingo)}\n213\t\n214\tRoboFlamingo uses a Perceiver Resampler with learnable cross-attention queries that aggregate visual features into a fixed set of tokens. Each query token is already an information-aggregated result, creating higher redundancy than MLP-processed tokens but less than pure linear projections.\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[h]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\caption{RoboFlamingo pruning curve on LIBERO-Object. Baseline SR: 42\\%. 500 episodes per $k$.}\n219\t\\label{tab:roboflamingo_curve}\n220\t\\small\n221\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n222\t\\toprule\n223\t$k$ & \\textbf{SR (\\%)} & $\\Delta$ \\textbf{(pp)} & \\textbf{Relative $\\Delta$} \\\\\n224\t\\midrule\n225\t1.00 & 42.0 & --- & --- \\\\\n226\t0.95 & 40.0 & $-2.0$ & $-4.8\\%$ \\\\\n227\t0.90 & 36.0 & $-6.0$ & $-14.3\\%$ \\\\\n228\t0.70 & 28.0 & $-14.0$ & $-33.3\\%$ \\\\\n229\t0.50 & 15.0 & $-27.0$ & $-64.3\\%$ \\\\\n230\t0.30 & 4.0 & $-38.0$ & $-90.5\\%$ \\\\\n231\t\\bottomrule\n232\t\\end{tabular}\n233\t\\end{table}\n234\t\n235\tThe degradation profile in Table~\\ref{tab:roboflamingo_curve} is gentler than OpenVLA's MLP curve at equivalent relative pruning levels. At $k=0.7$, RoboFlamingo retains 66.7\\% of baseline performance versus OpenVLA's 66.2\\%, but at $k=0.5$ RoboFlamingo retains only 35.7\\% versus OpenVLA's 50.5\\%. The Perceiver Resampler's CV of 0.11 sits between $\\pi_0$ (0.038) and X-VLA (0.151), filling a gap in the continuous spectrum and further validating CV as a predictor.\n236\t\n237\t\\subsection{Immune: Pure Linear Projection ($\\pi_0$)}\n238\t\n239\t$\\pi_0$ uses a single linear layer to project each vision patch to an LLM token, preserving the spatial independence of the vision encoder's output. Offline evaluation with normalized L1 loss reveals remarkable pruning immunity:\n240\t\\begin{itemize}\n241\t    \\item $k=0.1$ (90\\% removal): normalized loss $= 0.976$, a 2.4\\% \\emph{improvement}.\n242\t    \\item $k=0.5$ (50\\% removal): normalized loss $= 0.983$, still below unpruned.\n243\t\\end{itemize}\n244\t\n245\tThe key controlled comparison isolates the connector as the causal factor: $\\pi_0$ (256 tokens, linear) at $k=0.1$ \\emph{improves} by 2.4\\% while OpenVLA (256 tokens, 3-layer MLP) \\emph{degrades} by 61\\%. Same token count, same SigLIP encoder lineage, only the connector differs. $\\pi_0$'s importance CV of 0.038 (lowest in the spectrum) and attention entropy of 0.959 (near-maximum uniformity) confirm that linearly projected tokens carry independent, uniformly important information.\n246\t\n247\t%% ============================================================\n248\t%% 5. DIAGNOSE-THEN-COMPRESS FRAMEWORK\n249\t%% ============================================================\n250\t\\section{Diagnose-then-Compress Framework}\n251\t\n252\tThe taxonomy in Section~4 motivates a two-step framework: first diagnose a VLA's connector compressibility, then apply the appropriate compression strategy.\n253\t\n254\t\\subsection{Step 1: Connector Compressibility Diagnostic}\n255\t\n256\tWe propose the importance CV as a zero-cost diagnostic metric requiring only a single forward pass. Compute L2-norm importance scores for all visual tokens, then compute the coefficient of variation across tokens:\n257\t\\begin{equation}\n258\t\\text{CV} = \\frac{\\sigma(\\|\\mathbf{v}_i\\|_2)}{\\mu(\\|\\mathbf{v}_i\\|_2)}, \\quad i = 1, \\ldots, N\n259\t\\end{equation}\n260\twhere $\\mathbf{v}_i$ is the $i$-th visual token after the connector.\n261\t\n262\tThe CV spectrum predicts pruning behavior:\n263\t\\begin{itemize}\n264\t    \\item $\\text{CV} < 0.05$: Immune. Safe to prune aggressively (e.g., $\\pi_0$, CV${}=0.038$).\n265\t    \\item $0.05 \\leq \\text{CV} < 0.15$: Gentle to threshold-graded. Moderate pruning safe; cliff may exist (RoboFlamingo CV${}=0.11$, X-VLA CV${}=0.151$).\n266\t    \\item $0.15 \\leq \\text{CV} < 0.25$: Smooth graded. Pruning causes monotonic degradation; VTR recommended for aggressive compression (OpenVLA, CV${}=0.215$).\n267\t    \\item $\\text{CV} > 0.25$: Catastrophic risk. VTR required; hard pruning will likely fail (SmolVLA, CV${}=0.314$).\n268\t\\end{itemize}\n269\t\n270\tAs a supplementary tool, we propose a lightweight pruning probe: remove 5\\% of tokens and evaluate on a small sample. If performance drops by more than 5 absolute points, the connector is compression-vulnerable.\n271\t\n272\t\\subsection{Step 2: Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR)}\n273\t\n274\tFor connectors diagnosed as vulnerable, VTR provides learned compression. The module consists of $M$ learnable query vectors that attend to the $N$ output tokens of the vision connector via multi-head cross-attention:\n275\t\\begin{equation}\n276\t\\mathbf{Q}_{\\text{out}} = \\text{CrossAttn}(\\mathbf{Q}_{\\text{learn}}, \\mathbf{V}_{\\text{conn}}, \\mathbf{V}_{\\text{conn}})\n277\t\\end{equation}\n278\twhere $\\mathbf{Q}_{\\text{learn}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{M \\times d}$ are the learnable queries and $\\mathbf{V}_{\\text{conn}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times d}$ are the connector outputs. The module adds $\\sim$22M parameters and is trained with the LLM backbone frozen (VTC-only mode).\n279\t\n280\t\\subsubsection{VTR on SmolVLA (Pixel-Shuffle Connector)}\n281\t\n282\tTable~\\ref{tab:smolvla_vtr} presents VTR results on SmolVLA, where hard pruning at any ratio yields 0\\% SR.\n283\t\n284\t\\begin{table}[h]\n285\t\\centering\n286\t\\caption{SmolVLA VTR M-sweep (cross-attention, 3-seed mean$\\pm$std). Baseline: 44.8\\%. All hard pruning conditions: 0\\%. The M-curve is flat---all values within $\\pm$5pp.}\n287\t\\label{tab:smolvla_vtr}\n288\t\\small\n289\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n290\t\\toprule\n291\t$M$ & \\textbf{Compression} & \\textbf{SR (mean$\\pm$std)} & \\textbf{Seeds} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs baseline} \\\\\n292\t\\midrule\n293\t16 & 87.5\\% & 56.9$\\pm$1.3\\% & 42/43/44 & +12.1pp \\\\\n294\t32 & 75\\% & 60.1$\\pm$1.3\\% & 42/43/44 & +15.3pp \\\\\n295\t64 & 50\\% & 62.0$\\pm$2.3\\% & 42/43/44 & +17.2pp \\\\\n296\t96 & 25\\% & 54.5$\\pm$3.2\\% & 42/43/44 & +9.7pp \\\\\n297\t128 & 0\\% (passthrough) & 58.0$\\pm$3.5\\% & 42/43/44 & +13.2pp \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\end{table}\n301\t\n302\tThe M-curve is strikingly flat: M=16 (56.9\\%) $\\approx$ M=128 (58.0\\%), with a difference of only 1.1pp. This demonstrates that VTR achieves near-lossless compression at \\emph{any} ratio for pixel-shuffle connectors. The passthrough control (M=128, no compression) achieving +13.2pp above baseline proves that the gain comes from re-encoding itself---untangling spatial entanglement---rather than from compression.\n303\t\n304\tArchitecture independence is confirmed by an MLP adapter control ($\\sim$22.14M parameters) at M=32: mean SR $63.4\\pm2.6\\%$ vs.\\ cross-attention $60.1\\pm1.3\\%$ ($p=0.15$, not significant). Both architectures recover from 0\\% hard pruning, confirming that \\emph{any} learned re-encoding mechanism suffices.\n305\t\n306\tCross-suite generalization demonstrates that VTR trained on the Object suite transfers: M=32 achieves 53.2\\% on Spatial (vs.\\ 0.6\\% pruning cliff-edge) and 56.4\\% on Goal (vs.\\ 0\\% pruning).\n307\t\n308\t\\subsection{VTR Universality Across Connector Types}\n309\t\n310\tA critical question is whether VTR benefits only pixel-shuffle connectors or generalizes to other connector types. Table~\\ref{tab:vtr_universal} presents VTC-only results across SmolVLA and OpenVLA.\n311\t\n312\t\\begin{table}[h]\n313\t\\centering\n314\t\\caption{VTR cross-connector universality. VTC-only (frozen LLM, $\\sim$22M compressor). Hard pruning baselines at equivalent compression rates shown for comparison.}\n315\t\\label{tab:vtr_universal}\n316\t\\small\n317\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n318\t\\toprule\n319\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Connector} & $M$ & \\textbf{Compress.} & \\textbf{VTC SR} & \\textbf{Hard Prune SR} \\\\\n320\t\\midrule\n321\tSmolVLA & Pixel-shuffle & 32 & 75\\% & 60.1$\\pm$1.3\\% & 0\\% ($k{=}0.5$) \\\\\n322\tOpenVLA & 3-layer MLP & 32 & 87.5\\% & 45.0\\% & $\\sim$8\\% (est.) \\\\\n323\tOpenVLA & 3-layer MLP & 64 & 75\\% & 52.0\\% & 20.6\\% ($k{=}0.3$) \\\\\n324\tOpenVLA & 3-layer MLP & 96 & 62.5\\% & 58.0\\% & 37.4\\% ($k{=}0.5$) \\\\\n325\t\\bottomrule\n326\t\\end{tabular}\n327\t\\end{table}\n328\t\n329\tVTR provides substantial gains over hard pruning across both connector types:\n330\t\\begin{itemize}\n331\t    \\item \\textbf{Pixel-shuffle}: Recovery from 0\\% to 60.1\\% (+60.1pp). VTR here performs ``damage repair''---untangling spatial entanglement that hard pruning cannot overcome.\n332\t    \\item \\textbf{MLP projector}: At M=32 (87.5\\% compression), VTC achieves 45.0\\% vs.\\ $\\sim$8\\% estimated hard pruning at equivalent compression (+37pp). At M=64 (75\\% compression), 52.0\\% vs.\\ 20.6\\% at $k=0.3$ (+31.4pp). At M=96, 58.0\\% vs.\\ 37.4\\% at $k=0.5$ (+20.6pp).\n333\t\\end{itemize}\n334\t\n335\tThe OpenVLA M-curve is monotonically increasing (45$\\to$52$\\to$58\\% for M=32$\\to$64$\\to$96), contrasting with SmolVLA's flat M-curve. This difference is mechanistically interpretable: SmolVLA re-encoding repairs entanglement (effective at any $M$), while OpenVLA re-encoding performs information selection and aggregation from already-meaningful tokens (more queries $=$ more information retained).\n336\t\n337\tThe VTR ceiling depends on connector type: pixel-shuffle benefits most (recovery from complete failure), while MLP projector benefits substantially but cannot recover the full baseline due to information loss inherent in compression of already-independent tokens. This connector-dependent ceiling \\emph{strengthens} the architecture-dependency narrative---even the solution is shaped by the connector.\n338\t\n339\t%% ============================================================\n340\t%% 6. ANALYSIS\n341\t%% ============================================================\n342\t\\section{Analysis}\n343\t\n344\t\\subsection{Pruning Method Equivalence on MLP Projectors}\n345\t\n346\tA methodological concern in cross-model comparison is whether the choice of pruning method (random vs.\\ importance-based) biases results. Our primary evaluations use random pruning for OpenVLA and L2-norm importance-based pruning for X-VLA. To validate fair comparison, we conducted L2-norm pruning across all $k$ values on OpenVLA.\n347\t\n348\t\\begin{table}[h]\n349\t\\centering\n350\t\\caption{OpenVLA pruning method comparison: L2-norm importance-based vs.\\ random removal. All differences within $\\pm$3pp, confirming pruning method does not affect conclusions. 500 episodes per configuration.}\n351\t\\label{tab:pruning_method}\n352\t\\small\n353\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n354\t\\toprule\n355\t$k$ & \\textbf{Random SR} & \\textbf{L2-norm SR} & $\\Delta$ \\textbf{(pp)} & \\textbf{Within $\\pm$5pp?} \\\\\n356\t\\midrule\n357\t0.95 & 69.4\\% & 68.0\\% & $-1.4$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n358\t0.90 & 62.0\\% & 60.0\\% & $-2.0$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n359\t0.70 & 49.0\\% & 47.0\\% & $-2.0$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n360\t0.50 & 37.4\\% & 35.5\\% & $-1.9$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n361\t0.30 & 20.6\\% & 19.0\\% & $-1.6$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n362\t\\bottomrule\n363\t\\end{tabular}\n364\t\\end{table}\n365\t\n366\tTable~\\ref{tab:pruning_method} shows that L2-norm and random pruning produce equivalent results across all retention ratios (maximum $|\\Delta| = 2.0$pp, well within the $\\pm$5pp threshold). This equivalence is consistent with OpenVLA's moderate importance CV (0.215): the 3-layer MLP's non-linear mixing distributes information sufficiently uniformly that importance-based selection offers no advantage over random selection. This result eliminates pruning method discrepancy as a confound in cross-model comparisons and confirms that the taxonomy in Table~\\ref{tab:taxonomy} reflects connector architecture rather than methodological bias.\n367\t\n368\t\\subsection{Seed Robustness}\n369\t\n370\tTo verify that observed pruning patterns are architectural properties rather than evaluation artifacts, we replicate key configurations with alternative random seeds.\n371\t\n372\t\\begin{table}[h]\n373\t\\centering\n374\t\\caption{Seed robustness verification. All replications within expected ranges, confirming architectural patterns are seed-robust.}\n375\t\\label{tab:seed_robust}\n376\t\\small\n377\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n378\t\\toprule\n379\t\\textbf{Model} & $k$ & \\textbf{Seed A SR} & \\textbf{Seed B SR} & $\\Delta$ \\textbf{(pp)} & \\textbf{Pattern Preserved} \\\\\n380\t\\midrule\n381\tX-VLA & 0.9 & 97.6\\% (s42) & 96.0\\% (s44) & $-1.6$ & Immune \\checkmark \\\\\n382\tX-VLA & 0.7 & 56.2\\% (s42) & 52.0\\% (s44) & $-4.2$ & Cliff \\checkmark \\\\\n383\tOpenVLA & 0.7 & 49.0\\% (s7) & 47.0\\% (s43) & $-2.0$ & Graded \\checkmark \\\\\n384\tOpenVLA & 0.5 & 37.4\\% (s7) & 36.0\\% (s43) & $-1.4$ & Graded \\checkmark \\\\\n385\t\\bottomrule\n386\t\\end{tabular}\n387\t\\end{table}\n388\t\n389\tX-VLA's threshold-graded pattern is seed-robust: the cliff from $k=0.9$ to $k=0.7$ ($-44$pp in seed 42, $-44$pp in seed 44) is reproduced within $\\pm$6pp at each $k$ value. The absolute $k=0.7$ value varies by 4.2pp across seeds, reflecting per-task variance at the cliff point where some tasks sit near the decision boundary. Critically, the \\emph{pattern}---immune above $k=0.9$, cliff at $k=0.7$---is invariant.\n390\t\n391\tOpenVLA's smooth graded degradation is similarly robust: both $k=0.7$ and $k=0.5$ replicate within 2pp across seeds, confirming the monotonic degradation profile.\n392\t\n393\t\\subsection{Re-encoding Mechanism Analysis}\n394\t\n395\tThree lines of evidence identify re-encoding as the causal mechanism in VTR's effectiveness on SmolVLA:\n396\t\n397\t\\textbf{(1) Passthrough control.} M=128 (no compression, passthrough) achieves 58.0$\\pm$3.5\\% SR---+13.2pp above the 44.8\\% baseline. Since no tokens are removed, this gain comes entirely from the re-encoding transformation.\n398\t\n399\t\\textbf{(2) Architecture independence.} MLP adapter (63.4$\\pm$2.6\\%) and cross-attention (60.1$\\pm$1.3\\%) at M=32 show no significant difference ($p=0.15$, Welch's $t$-test). Two architectures achieving comparable recovery confirms the mechanism is re-encoding, not architecture-specific."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"3d0d62da-abd1-4465-bc36-82b21ffd4f73","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:20.603Z"}
