{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016HzBHqaKY2ER8Gsyo1LuJx","type":"tool_result","content":"398\t\n399\t\\textbf{(2) Architecture independence.} MLP adapter (63.4$\\pm$2.6\\%) and cross-attention (60.1$\\pm$1.3\\%) at M=32 show no significant difference ($p=0.15$, Welch's $t$-test). Two architectures achieving comparable recovery confirms the mechanism is re-encoding, not architecture-specific.\n400\t\n401\t\\textbf{(3) Flat M-curve.} All 5 $M$ values (16, 32, 64, 96, 128) produce SR within $\\pm$5pp of each other, indicating that compression ratio does not affect performance for pixel-shuffle connectors. The re-encoding layer untangles spatial information regardless of how many output queries are used.\n402\t\n403\tThe M=96 dip (54.5$\\pm$3.2\\%) is statistically confirmed ($p=0.034$ vs.\\ M=64), likely reflecting a suboptimal training configuration rather than a fundamental property, given that both lower ($M=64$) and higher ($M=128$) values achieve higher SR.\n404\t\n405\t\\subsection{Connector-Dependent VTR Mechanisms}\n406\t\n407\tVTR operates through fundamentally different mechanisms depending on connector type:\n408\t\n409\t\\textbf{Pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA):} Re-encoding performs \\emph{spatial disentanglement}. The cross-attention queries learn to aggregate information across spatially entangled tokens, reconstructing coherent visual features from the channel-folded representations. This is a repair operation---the information exists but is inaccessible to the LLM in its entangled form. The flat M-curve confirms that even 16 queries suffice to untangle 128 entangled tokens.\n410\t\n411\t\\textbf{MLP projector (OpenVLA):} Re-encoding performs \\emph{information selection and aggregation}. Each MLP-projected token is already individually meaningful (CV=0.215, moderate heterogeneity), so the cross-attention queries must select and compress information rather than repair it. The monotonically increasing M-curve (45$\\to$52$\\to$58\\% for M=32$\\to$64$\\to$96) reflects this: more queries capture more of the distributed information. The gains over hard pruning (+31--37pp at equivalent compression) demonstrate that learned selection substantially outperforms uninformed removal, even when tokens are individually interpretable.\n412\t\n413\t%% ============================================================\n414\t%% 7. DISCUSSION\n415\t%% ============================================================\n416\t\\section{Discussion}\n417\t\n418\t\\textbf{Design implications.} Our findings provide concrete guidance for VLA architecture design. Pixel-shuffle connectors should be avoided if inference-time token reduction is desired, unless accompanied by a learned re-encoding module. Linear projections naturally support aggressive pruning, and practitioners can verify tolerance using the zero-cost CV diagnostic before deployment. For MLP-projector VLAs, VTR provides a practical path to 75--87.5\\% token compression with substantial efficiency gains (75\\% KV-cache reduction at M=32).\n419\t\n420\t\\textbf{Relationship to concurrent work.} Four concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) propose token pruning for MLP-projector VLAs. Their success on these architectures is consistent with our taxonomy---MLP connectors support graded pruning. However, none test on pixel-shuffle or analyze \\emph{why} pruning works. Our framework explains their results and predicts their failure modes.\n421\t\n422\t\\textbf{Limitations.} All closed-loop evaluations use the LIBERO simulation benchmark. Real-robot validation is needed to confirm transfer. $\\pi_0$ is evaluated offline (normalized loss) rather than closed-loop due to its flow-matching action head. RoboFlamingo's lower absolute baseline (42\\%) may compress the dynamic range of degradation. The VTR module adds 22M parameters and requires fine-tuning, limiting applicability for zero-shot deployment.\n423\t\n424\t%% ============================================================\n425\t%% 8. CONCLUSION\n426\t%% ============================================================\n427\t\\section{Conclusion}\n428\t\n429\tWe present the first systematic study of token pruning robustness across VLA architectures, discovering that the effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines compressibility along a continuous spectrum. Testing 6 models across 4 connector families, we establish a four-class taxonomy: immune (pure linear, $\\pi_0$), gentle to threshold-graded (Perceiver Resampler, linear+BART), smooth graded (MLP), and catastrophic (pixel-shuffle). The importance CV serves as a zero-cost predictor of this spectrum. Our Diagnose-then-Compress framework pairs this diagnostic with VTR, a universal re-encoding module that recovers pixel-shuffle from 0\\% to 60\\%+ SR and provides 45--58\\% SR on MLP projectors at 75--87.5\\% compression. VTR's universality across connector types, combined with the diagnostic's predictive power, provides practitioners with both the understanding and tools to efficiently deploy any VLA architecture.\n430\t\n431\t\\bibliography{references}\n432\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n433\t\n434\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n435\t\\bibitem{brohan2023rt2}\n436\tA.~Brohan et al.\n437\t\\newblock RT-2: Vision-language-action models transfer web knowledge to robotic control.\n438\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n439\t\n440\t\\bibitem{kim2024openvla}\n441\tM.~J. Kim et al.\n442\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n443\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n444\t\n445\t\\bibitem{black2024pi0}\n446\tK.~Black et al.\n447\t\\newblock $\\pi_0$: A vision-language-action flow model for general robot control.\n448\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.24164}, 2024.\n449\t\n450\t\\bibitem{zhu2025smolvla}\n451\tF.~Zhu et al.\n452\t\\newblock SmolVLA: A small vision-language-action model for efficient robot learning.\n453\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem{xvla2025}\n456\tX-VLA Team.\n457\t\\newblock X-VLA: Cross-embodiment vision-language-action model.\n458\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem{roboflamingo2024}\n461\tB.~Li et al.\n462\t\\newblock Vision-language foundation models as effective robot imitators.\n463\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem{libero2023}\n466\tB.~Liu et al.\n467\t\\newblock LIBERO: Benchmarking knowledge transfer for lifelong robot learning.\n468\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n469\t\n470\t\\bibitem{fastv2024}\n471\tL.~Chen et al.\n472\t\\newblock An image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2: Plug-and-play inference acceleration for large vision-language models.\n473\t\\newblock In \\emph{ECCV}, 2024.\n474\t\n475\t\\bibitem{h2o2023}\n476\tZ.~Zhang et al.\n477\t\\newblock H2O: Heavy-hitter oracle for efficient generative inference of large language models.\n478\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem{tome2023}\n481\tD.~Bolya et al.\n482\t\\newblock Token merging: Your ViT but faster.\n483\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n484\t\\end{thebibliography}\n485\t\n486\t\\end{document}\n487\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"7768f29a-4bf4-4754-aa87-448498369b49","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:25.716Z"}
