{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvvYWWcHHFJVsWidd3TEto","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the context needed. Let me compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined cross-architecture comparison.** Testing 6 VLA models across 4 connector families is a genuinely novel experimental design. No prior work has systematically compared token pruning robustness across VLA architectures. The controlled comparison between pi0 (256 tokens, linear) and OpenVLA (256 tokens, MLP) at matched token counts is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper, cleanly isolating the connector as the causal variable.\n\n- **Thorough SmolVLA ablation.** The cliff-edge finding is convincingly established through multiple independent axes: 3 scoring methods (ACIS, random, geometric/Sobel), 3 retention ratios, 3 pruning mechanisms (zero-mask, true removal, ToMe), 2 suites beyond Object (Spatial, Goal), and official checkpoint replication. This is the most rigorous single-architecture result in the paper.\n\n- **True token removal protocol.** The choice of true token removal over zero-masking is well-justified and methodologically sound — zero-masking would confound results by preserving positional information and KV-cache size, defeating the purpose of a compression study.\n\n- **VTR mechanism isolation.** The three-pronged analysis (M=128 passthrough control, MLP adapter vs. cross-attention architecture comparison, flat M-curve) is a strong experimental design for establishing re-encoding as the causal mechanism. The passthrough control is particularly valuable — it cleanly separates the re-encoding effect from the compression effect.\n\n- **CV as a continuous predictor.** The importance CV metric is simple, zero-cost, and forms a monotonic spectrum across 5 data points (0.038, 0.11, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314). The X-VLA CV=0.151 correctly predicting intermediate pruning behavior (rather than pure immunity as the \"linear projection\" label might suggest) is a compelling validation.\n\n- **Pruning method equivalence analysis (Table 8).** Demonstrating that L2-norm and random pruning produce equivalent results on OpenVLA (max |delta|=2.0pp) directly addresses the cross-model methodology discrepancy and strengthens the architecture-dependency claim.\n\n- **Statistical reporting.** Wilson 95% CIs for closed-loop results, 3-seed mean +/- std for VTR M-sweep, Welch's t-tests for pairwise comparisons — the statistical framework is appropriate for the sample sizes used.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Heterogeneous evaluation modes are a significant confound.** pi0 is evaluated offline (normalized L1 loss), OpenVLA-OFT only contributes entropy data, and the remaining 4 models use closed-loop SR. The paper's central claim is a continuous taxonomy across 6 models, but only 4 are measured on the same metric. Offline loss and closed-loop SR are fundamentally different quantities — a 2.4% loss improvement at k=0.1 for pi0 does not guarantee closed-loop immunity. The paper acknowledges this in the limitations section but understates its impact on the taxonomy's validity. The \"immune\" classification for pi0 rests entirely on offline evidence.\n\n- **Seed robustness is thin.** Only 2 seeds for 4 configurations (X-VLA k=0.7/0.9, OpenVLA k=0.7/0.5). The X-VLA k=0.7 result shows 4.2pp variance across seeds (56.2% vs 52.0%), which is non-trivial for a cliff point. The OpenVLA main curve (Table 3) appears to be single-seed. For a taxonomy paper where the exact shape of degradation curves is the core contribution, 2-seed replication at selected points is insufficient. The ROADMAP reveals X-VLA seed43 k=0.7 is actually showing ~68.3% — a 12pp variance from the seed42 result of 56.2% — which, if confirmed, would substantially weaken the \"cliff at k=0.7\" characterization.\n\n- **RoboFlamingo results are under-specified and the baseline is problematic.** The 42% baseline SR is low, compressing the observable degradation range. The paper_framing.md notes RoboFlamingo was \"partially covered\" and originally a \"bonus.\" Table 5 data (42% -> 28% -> 15% -> 4%) lacks confidence intervals, seed information, and methodological details that other models receive. The CV=0.11 data point is critical for the continuous spectrum claim but rests on the weakest experimental foundation.\n\n- **\"Universal\" VTR claim is overstated.** VTR is tested on exactly 2 connector types (pixel-shuffle and MLP). It is not tested on linear projection (pi0, X-VLA) or Perceiver Resampler (RoboFlamingo). Furthermore, the OpenVLA VTC-only results in Table 7 appear to be single-seed (no +/- std reported), contrasting with the 3-seed SmolVLA results. The paper_framing.md reveals that full VTR on OpenVLA (M=128) produced 0% SR due to overfitting, and the \"VTC-only\" results for M=32/64/96 were still in progress at time of writing. The provenance of the Table 7 OpenVLA numbers needs clarification.\n\n- **VTR adds 22M parameters and requires fine-tuning — comparison to parameter-free alternatives is missing.** The paper compares VTR only against hard pruning (the worst-case baseline). No comparison against parameter-free methods (ToMe, FastV, attention-guided pruning) at equivalent compression ratios on OpenVLA. For SmolVLA, the all-methods-fail result justifies VTR, but for OpenVLA the comparison is incomplete. The 6% inference speedup at M=32 is modest and the 75% KV-cache reduction, while significant, is only mentioned in supplementary.\n\n- **Sample size adequacy.** 500 episodes (50 per task x 10 tasks) with Wilson CIs is reasonable for detecting large effects (cliff-edge, >10pp differences). However, for distinguishing between adjacent CV classes or establishing the exact cliff location, 50 episodes per task introduces substantial per-task noise. X-VLA k=0.7 per-task SRs range from 0% to 100% — this extreme variance suggests 50 episodes per task is insufficient to characterize task-level behavior at boundary conditions.\n\n- **OpenVLA-OFT contributes only entropy data.** The ROADMAP confirms a confirmed eval script bug (k=1.0 = 0% SR) that invalidated all OFT pruning data. The paper retains OFT only for the entropy comparison (0.667 vs 0.661), but this makes the \"6 models\" claim somewhat misleading — OFT does not contribute pruning data to the taxonomy.\n\n- **The M=96 dip in SmolVLA VTR is unexplained.** The paper acknowledges the dip is statistically significant (p=0.034 vs M=64) but attributes it to \"suboptimal training configuration\" without evidence. This undermines the \"flat M-curve\" claim. If the M-curve has a statistically significant non-monotonicity, it is not flat.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The ROADMAP indicates X-VLA seed43 k=0.7 is showing ~68.3% SR versus seed42's 56.2% — a 12pp difference. If this holds, how does it affect the \"cliff at k=0.7\" characterization? Would you reclassify X-VLA as \"gentle graded\" rather than \"threshold-graded\"?\n\n2. For pi0, have you considered running even a small-scale closed-loop evaluation (e.g., 100 episodes at k=0.5 and k=0.3) to validate the offline-to-closed-loop transfer of the \"immune\" classification? The entire left anchor of the CV spectrum depends on offline-only evidence.\n\n3. Table 7 reports OpenVLA VTC-only results at M=32 (45%), M=64 (52%), M=96 (58%) without error bars. Are these single-seed results? If so, what is the justification for the asymmetric reporting standard vs. SmolVLA's 3-seed results?\n\n4. The paper claims the CV spectrum is a \"continuous predictor,\" but with only 5 data points, how would you distinguish a continuous relationship from 5 discrete categories that happen to be ordered? What is the prediction for a hypothetical connector with CV=0.18?\n\n5. RoboFlamingo CV=0.11 and entropy=0.88 are reported in Table 2 but not discussed in the diagnostic metrics section. How were these computed? RoboFlamingo's Perceiver Resampler produces a fixed number of output tokens regardless of input — does the CV metric apply in the same way to cross-attention-aggregated tokens vs. independently projected tokens?\n\n6. The VTR passthrough (M=128) on SmolVLA achieves 58.0% — substantially above the 44.8% baseline without VTR. This implies VTR is not just compressing but actively improving the representation. Does this mean the SmolVLA baseline is undertrained, or is there another explanation for why adding 22M parameters and re-encoding improves over the uncompressed baseline?\n\n7. Why was ToMe not tested as a VTR alternative on OpenVLA? ToMe is parameter-free and performs learned-like merging. Its inclusion would strengthen the claim that VTR's 22M parameter cost is justified.\n\n8. The paper notes pruning method discrepancy: OpenVLA uses random removal while X-VLA uses L2-norm importance-based removal for the main taxonomy (Table 2). Although Table 8 shows equivalence on OpenVLA, has L2-norm vs. random equivalence been verified on X-VLA? X-VLA's lower CV (0.151) might show a different pattern.\n\n### Methods Score: 6/10\n\nThe experimental methodology is generally sound, with appropriate benchmarks, reasonable sample sizes, and correct statistical frameworks. The core comparison framework (cross-architecture, controlled variables) is well-designed. However, the heterogeneous evaluation modes (offline vs. closed-loop) for the taxonomy's anchor points, the thin seed robustness analysis (especially concerning given emerging evidence of 12pp variance at the X-VLA cliff), and the incomplete VTR evaluation across connector types weaken the methodological rigor. The CV diagnostic, while promising, is validated on only 5 points with no out-of-sample prediction. The methods are adequate for a workshop paper but need strengthening for a top venue.\n\n### Experiments Score: 6/10\n\nThe experimental coverage is broad — 6 models, multiple pruning methods, VTR with M-sweep and architecture controls. The SmolVLA cliff-edge result is thoroughly established and the pruning method equivalence analysis is a valuable contribution. However, several critical gaps reduce the score: (1) the pi0 \"immune\" anchor relies entirely on offline evaluation, (2) RoboFlamingo data quality is below the standard of other models, (3) OpenVLA VTR results lack multi-seed replication, (4) the \"universal\" VTR claim covers only 2 of 4 connector types, (5) OFT contributes no pruning data due to a confirmed bug, and (6) the emerging seed variance for X-VLA k=0.7 threatens the cliff characterization. The 500-episode protocol is adequate for the coarse taxonomy but insufficient for the fine-grained claims (exact cliff location, continuous CV prediction). The paper would benefit from 3-seed replication of all main curves and closed-loop evaluation of pi0 at even one pruning level."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4d66be69a00477f2 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4d66be69a00477f2' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 64108\ntool_uses: 14\nduration_ms: 117282</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"d9f6b1f1-96b7-4011-9890-21ed67caee21","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:39:48.542Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents a systematic cross-architecture study of visual token pruning robustness across 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families. Key contributions:\n1. A four-class taxonomy of pruning behavior: immune (π0, pure linear), gentle graded (RoboFlamingo, Perceiver Resampler), threshold-graded (X-VLA, linear+BART), smooth graded (OpenVLA, MLP), catastrophic (SmolVLA, pixel-shuffle).\n2. Importance CV as a zero-cost diagnostic predictor of pruning tolerance (CV spectrum: 0.038→0.11→0.151→0.215→0.314).\n3. A \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" framework with Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a ~22M parameter cross-attention module that recovers pixel-shuffle from 0% to 60.1% SR at 75% compression, and achieves 45-58% SR on MLP projectors at 75-87.5% compression.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- Benchmark: LIBERO manipulation benchmark, 500 episodes per configuration (50 per task × 10 tasks), Wilson 95% CI\n- Pruning: true token removal (not zero-masking) at k ∈ {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95, 1.0}\n- 6 models: π0 (3.3B, pure linear, 256 tokens, offline eval), RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler, closed-loop), X-VLA (880M, linear+BART 12L, 50 tokens, closed-loop), OpenVLA (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens, closed-loop), OpenVLA-OFT (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens, offline eval), SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle 16×, 128 tokens, closed-loop)\n- VTR: VTC-only (frozen LLM) and full VTR variants; M-sweep for SmolVLA (M=16,32,64,96,128) with 3-seed mean±std\n\n## Key Results Tables\nTable 2 (Taxonomy): CV spectrum 0.038→0.11→0.151→0.215→0.314 maps to immune→gentle→threshold→smooth→catastrophic\nTable 3 (OpenVLA pruning): k=1.0: 74%, k=0.95: 69.4%, k=0.9: 62%, k=0.7: 49%, k=0.5: 37.4%, k=0.3: 20.6%\nTable 4 (X-VLA): k=1.0: 99.2%, k=0.9: 97.6%, k=0.7: 56.2% (cliff), k=0.5: 42.6%, k=0.3: 23%\nTable 5 (RoboFlamingo): Baseline 42%, k=0.7: 28%, k=0.5: 15%, k=0.3: 4%\nTable 6 (SmolVLA VTR M-sweep): All M values within ±5pp, M=32: 60.1±1.3%, flat M-curve\nTable 7 (VTR universality): SmolVLA M=32: 60.1±1.3% vs 0% hard prune; OpenVLA M=32: 45% vs ~8%; M=64: 52% vs 20.6%; M=96: 58% vs 37.4%\nTable 8 (L2-norm vs random): max |Δ|=2.0pp across all k values\nTable 9 (Seed robustness): X-VLA k=0.7 seed42: 56.2% vs seed44: 52.0% (Δ=-4.2pp); OpenVLA k=0.7: 49% vs 47% (Δ=-2pp)\n\n## Imagined Experiments from Design\nThe experimental design includes 12 imagined experiments. The paper covers:\n- OpenVLA VTC-only M=32/64/96 (3 experiments) ✓\n- OpenVLA L2-norm pruning at k=0.95/0.9/0.7/0.5/0.3 (5 experiments) ✓\n- X-VLA seed44 replication at k=0.7 and k=0.9 (2 experiments) ✓\n- OpenVLA random pruning seed43 at k=0.7 and k=0.5 (2 experiments) ✓\n- RoboFlamingo Perceiver Resampler baseline + pruning (1 experiment) — partially covered (pruning curve shown but noted as bonus)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Methodology Assessment\n- Is the experimental methodology sound? Are the evaluation protocols appropriate?\n- Is the pruning protocol (true removal vs masking) well justified?\n- Is the CV diagnostic metric well-motivated and rigorously validated?\n- Is VTR's design (cross-attention re-encoding) well-justified against alternatives?\n- Are there methodological concerns (e.g., different eval modes for different models: offline for π0 and OFT, closed-loop for others)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are sample sizes sufficient (500 episodes per config)?\n- Is the seed robustness analysis adequate (only 2 seeds for a few configs)?\n- Are confidence intervals and statistical tests reported appropriately?\n- Are the VTR results convincing (3 seeds for SmolVLA, but single seed for OpenVLA VTC)?\n- Is the comparison fair across models with very different baselines (π0 99.2% vs RoboFlamingo 42%)?\n\n### 3. Completeness and Coverage\n- Does the paper cover the planned experiments adequately?\n- Are there missing ablations or experiments that would strengthen claims?\n- Is the RoboFlamingo experiment convincingly presented?\n\n### 4. Specific Concerns\n- π0 is evaluated offline (normalized L1 loss) not closed-loop. Is this a significant limitation?\n- RoboFlamingo baseline is 42% — does this low baseline limit interpretability?\n- The paper claims VTR is \"universal\" but only tests on 2 connector types\n- VTR adds 22M parameters and requires fine-tuning — how does this compare to parameter-free alternatives?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)  \n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Methods Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Experiments Score** (1-10, with justification)","agentId":"a4d66be69a00477f2","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the context needed. Let me compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined cross-architecture comparison.** Testing 6 VLA models across 4 connector families is a genuinely novel experimental design. No prior work has systematically compared token pruning robustness across VLA architectures. The controlled comparison between pi0 (256 tokens, linear) and OpenVLA (256 tokens, MLP) at matched token counts is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper, cleanly isolating the connector as the causal variable.\n\n- **Thorough SmolVLA ablation.** The cliff-edge finding is convincingly established through multiple independent axes: 3 scoring methods (ACIS, random, geometric/Sobel), 3 retention ratios, 3 pruning mechanisms (zero-mask, true removal, ToMe), 2 suites beyond Object (Spatial, Goal), and official checkpoint replication. This is the most rigorous single-architecture result in the paper.\n\n- **True token removal protocol.** The choice of true token removal over zero-masking is well-justified and methodologically sound — zero-masking would confound results by preserving positional information and KV-cache size, defeating the purpose of a compression study.\n\n- **VTR mechanism isolation.** The three-pronged analysis (M=128 passthrough control, MLP adapter vs. cross-attention architecture comparison, flat M-curve) is a strong experimental design for establishing re-encoding as the causal mechanism. The passthrough control is particularly valuable — it cleanly separates the re-encoding effect from the compression effect.\n\n- **CV as a continuous predictor.** The importance CV metric is simple, zero-cost, and forms a monotonic spectrum across 5 data points (0.038, 0.11, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314). The X-VLA CV=0.151 correctly predicting intermediate pruning behavior (rather than pure immunity as the \"linear projection\" label might suggest) is a compelling validation.\n\n- **Pruning method equivalence analysis (Table 8).** Demonstrating that L2-norm and random pruning produce equivalent results on OpenVLA (max |delta|=2.0pp) directly addresses the cross-model methodology discrepancy and strengthens the architecture-dependency claim.\n\n- **Statistical reporting.** Wilson 95% CIs for closed-loop results, 3-seed mean +/- std for VTR M-sweep, Welch's t-tests for pairwise comparisons — the statistical framework is appropriate for the sample sizes used.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Heterogeneous evaluation modes are a significant confound.** pi0 is evaluated offline (normalized L1 loss), OpenVLA-OFT only contributes entropy data, and the remaining 4 models use closed-loop SR. The paper's central claim is a continuous taxonomy across 6 models, but only 4 are measured on the same metric. Offline loss and closed-loop SR are fundamentally different quantities — a 2.4% loss improvement at k=0.1 for pi0 does not guarantee closed-loop immunity. The paper acknowledges this in the limitations section but understates its impact on the taxonomy's validity. The \"immune\" classification for pi0 rests entirely on offline evidence.\n\n- **Seed robustness is thin.** Only 2 seeds for 4 configurations (X-VLA k=0.7/0.9, OpenVLA k=0.7/0.5). The X-VLA k=0.7 result shows 4.2pp variance across seeds (56.2% vs 52.0%), which is non-trivial for a cliff point. The OpenVLA main curve (Table 3) appears to be single-seed. For a taxonomy paper where the exact shape of degradation curves is the core contribution, 2-seed replication at selected points is insufficient. The ROADMAP reveals X-VLA seed43 k=0.7 is actually showing ~68.3% — a 12pp variance from the seed42 result of 56.2% — which, if confirmed, would substantially weaken the \"cliff at k=0.7\" characterization.\n\n- **RoboFlamingo results are under-specified and the baseline is problematic.** The 42% baseline SR is low, compressing the observable degradation range. The paper_framing.md notes RoboFlamingo was \"partially covered\" and originally a \"bonus.\" Table 5 data (42% -> 28% -> 15% -> 4%) lacks confidence intervals, seed information, and methodological details that other models receive. The CV=0.11 data point is critical for the continuous spectrum claim but rests on the weakest experimental foundation.\n\n- **\"Universal\" VTR claim is overstated.** VTR is tested on exactly 2 connector types (pixel-shuffle and MLP). It is not tested on linear projection (pi0, X-VLA) or Perceiver Resampler (RoboFlamingo). Furthermore, the OpenVLA VTC-only results in Table 7 appear to be single-seed (no +/- std reported), contrasting with the 3-seed SmolVLA results. The paper_framing.md reveals that full VTR on OpenVLA (M=128) produced 0% SR due to overfitting, and the \"VTC-only\" results for M=32/64/96 were still in progress at time of writing. The provenance of the Table 7 OpenVLA numbers needs clarification.\n\n- **VTR adds 22M parameters and requires fine-tuning — comparison to parameter-free alternatives is missing.** The paper compares VTR only against hard pruning (the worst-case baseline). No comparison against parameter-free methods (ToMe, FastV, attention-guided pruning) at equivalent compression ratios on OpenVLA. For SmolVLA, the all-methods-fail result justifies VTR, but for OpenVLA the comparison is incomplete. The 6% inference speedup at M=32 is modest and the 75% KV-cache reduction, while significant, is only mentioned in supplementary.\n\n- **Sample size adequacy.** 500 episodes (50 per task x 10 tasks) with Wilson CIs is reasonable for detecting large effects (cliff-edge, >10pp differences). However, for distinguishing between adjacent CV classes or establishing the exact cliff location, 50 episodes per task introduces substantial per-task noise. X-VLA k=0.7 per-task SRs range from 0% to 100% — this extreme variance suggests 50 episodes per task is insufficient to characterize task-level behavior at boundary conditions.\n\n- **OpenVLA-OFT contributes only entropy data.** The ROADMAP confirms a confirmed eval script bug (k=1.0 = 0% SR) that invalidated all OFT pruning data. The paper retains OFT only for the entropy comparison (0.667 vs 0.661), but this makes the \"6 models\" claim somewhat misleading — OFT does not contribute pruning data to the taxonomy.\n\n- **The M=96 dip in SmolVLA VTR is unexplained.** The paper acknowledges the dip is statistically significant (p=0.034 vs M=64) but attributes it to \"suboptimal training configuration\" without evidence. This undermines the \"flat M-curve\" claim. If the M-curve has a statistically significant non-monotonicity, it is not flat.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The ROADMAP indicates X-VLA seed43 k=0.7 is showing ~68.3% SR versus seed42's 56.2% — a 12pp difference. If this holds, how does it affect the \"cliff at k=0.7\" characterization? Would you reclassify X-VLA as \"gentle graded\" rather than \"threshold-graded\"?\n\n2. For pi0, have you considered running even a small-scale closed-loop evaluation (e.g., 100 episodes at k=0.5 and k=0.3) to validate the offline-to-closed-loop transfer of the \"immune\" classification? The entire left anchor of the CV spectrum depends on offline-only evidence.\n\n3. Table 7 reports OpenVLA VTC-only results at M=32 (45%), M=64 (52%), M=96 (58%) without error bars. Are these single-seed results? If so, what is the justification for the asymmetric reporting standard vs. SmolVLA's 3-seed results?\n\n4. The paper claims the CV spectrum is a \"continuous predictor,\" but with only 5 data points, how would you distinguish a continuous relationship from 5 discrete categories that happen to be ordered? What is the prediction for a hypothetical connector with CV=0.18?\n\n5. RoboFlamingo CV=0.11 and entropy=0.88 are reported in Table 2 but not discussed in the diagnostic metrics section. How were these computed? RoboFlamingo's Perceiver Resampler produces a fixed number of output tokens regardless of input — does the CV metric apply in the same way to cross-attention-aggregated tokens vs. independently projected tokens?\n\n6. The VTR passthrough (M=128) on SmolVLA achieves 58.0% — substantially above the 44.8% baseline without VTR. This implies VTR is not just compressing but actively improving the representation. Does this mean the SmolVLA baseline is undertrained, or is there another explanation for why adding 22M parameters and re-encoding improves over the uncompressed baseline?\n\n7. Why was ToMe not tested as a VTR alternative on OpenVLA? ToMe is parameter-free and performs learned-like merging. Its inclusion would strengthen the claim that VTR's 22M parameter cost is justified.\n\n8. The paper notes pruning method discrepancy: OpenVLA uses random removal while X-VLA uses L2-norm importance-based removal for the main taxonomy (Table 2). Although Table 8 shows equivalence on OpenVLA, has L2-norm vs. random equivalence been verified on X-VLA? X-VLA's lower CV (0.151) might show a different pattern.\n\n### Methods Score: 6/10\n\nThe experimental methodology is generally sound, with appropriate benchmarks, reasonable sample sizes, and correct statistical frameworks. The core comparison framework (cross-architecture, controlled variables) is well-designed. However, the heterogeneous evaluation modes (offline vs. closed-loop) for the taxonomy's anchor points, the thin seed robustness analysis (especially concerning given emerging evidence of 12pp variance at the X-VLA cliff), and the incomplete VTR evaluation across connector types weaken the methodological rigor. The CV diagnostic, while promising, is validated on only 5 points with no out-of-sample prediction. The methods are adequate for a workshop paper but need strengthening for a top venue.\n\n### Experiments Score: 6/10\n\nThe experimental coverage is broad — 6 models, multiple pruning methods, VTR with M-sweep and architecture controls. The SmolVLA cliff-edge result is thoroughly established and the pruning method equivalence analysis is a valuable contribution. However, several critical gaps reduce the score: (1) the pi0 \"immune\" anchor relies entirely on offline evaluation, (2) RoboFlamingo data quality is below the standard of other models, (3) OpenVLA VTR results lack multi-seed replication, (4) the \"universal\" VTR claim covers only 2 of 4 connector types, (5) OFT contributes no pruning data due to a confirmed bug, and (6) the emerging seed variance for X-VLA k=0.7 threatens the cliff characterization. The 500-episode protocol is adequate for the coarse taxonomy but insufficient for the fine-grained claims (exact cliff location, continuous CV prediction). The paper would benefit from 3-seed replication of all main curves and closed-loop evaluation of pi0 at even one pruning level."}],"totalDurationMs":117282,"totalTokens":64108,"totalToolUseCount":14,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":742,"cache_read_input_tokens":60563,"output_tokens":2800,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":742},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2800,"cache_read_input_tokens":60563,"cache_creation_input_tokens":742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":742,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aa1d2550616220eb3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01DE8KKbTR1R3Uc24fbjeCTk","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":68029,"tool_uses":18,"duration_ms":147396},"uuid":"64032006-2083-4c49-b776-87550d0e6f14","session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DE8KKbTR1R3Uc24fbjeCTk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental evidence, the project's internal decision log, the full paper tex, the literature map, and the framing document. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review of \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work/Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined research question with clean experimental design.** The paper asks a specific, answerable question — why does token pruning work on some VLAs but not others? — and systematically tests it across 6 models. The controlled comparison between pi0 (256 tokens, linear) and OpenVLA (256 tokens, MLP) is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper, genuinely isolating connector type from model size and token count.\n\n- **The four-class taxonomy is descriptively useful.** Even if the taxonomy is ultimately empirical, it provides the community with a concrete vocabulary and reference table for reasoning about token compression across architectures. The progression from immune to catastrophic, with two intermediate classes, is more nuanced than a simple binary \"works/doesn't work.\"\n\n- **Actionable practitioner guidance.** The CV diagnostic (one forward pass, zero cost) is immediately usable. A robotics engineer considering token pruning for a new VLA deployment can compute CV and get a calibrated expectation of pruning tolerance. This is the kind of tool CoRL reviewers value.\n\n- **SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is striking.** The demonstration that removing just 5% of tokens (7/128) from a pixel-shuffle connector yields 0% SR is a genuinely surprising and important negative result. The fact that this holds across all pruning strategies (random, ACIS, ToMe, geometric priors) and across task suites makes it robust.\n\n- **VTR recovery from 0% to 60%+ on pixel-shuffle is convincing.** The flat M-curve (M=16 through M=128 all within ±5pp) combined with architecture independence (cross-attention vs MLP adapter, p=0.15) provides strong evidence that re-encoding is the causal mechanism, not a specific architecture or compression ratio.\n\n- **Honest treatment of negative results.** The paper reports VTR's failure on OpenVLA MLP projector (0% SR at M=128) and reframes it as a connector-dependency finding rather than hiding it. The OFT eval bug is acknowledged. This builds credibility.\n\n- **RoboFlamingo inclusion fills a gap.** Adding the Perceiver Resampler as a \"gentle graded\" class (CV=0.11) strengthens the continuous spectrum narrative beyond the original 3-class structure.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **CV as \"continuous predictor\" is validated on only 5 data points.** The paper claims CV forms a continuous spectrum predicting pruning tolerance, but this is fitted to exactly 5 models (pi0=0.038, RoboFlamingo=0.11, X-VLA=0.151, OpenVLA=0.215, SmolVLA=0.314). With 5 data points, any monotonic function would appear to \"predict\" behavior. The thresholds proposed (CV<0.05 = immune, 0.05-0.15 = gentle, etc.) are post-hoc bin boundaries drawn around individual data points rather than validated decision boundaries. This is the paper's most overclaimed contribution. At minimum, the paper should acknowledge this is a hypothesis generated from 5 observations, not a validated predictor.\n\n- **\"First systematic cross-architecture study\" claim needs qualification.** The claim is likely true in the narrow sense of token *pruning* across VLA architectures specifically. However, cross-architecture efficiency comparisons in VLMs more broadly are not new — EfficientVLA (Yang et al. 2025) tests on CogACT, LLaVA-PruMerge tests across LLaVA variants, and the RoboVLMs framework (2024) systematically compares 8+ VLM backbones. The paper should sharpen this claim to \"first study specifically isolating the connector's role in determining pruning robustness.\"\n\n- **VTR is not architecturally novel.** Cross-attention re-encoding with learnable queries is precisely the mechanism of Q-Former (BLIP-2, Li et al. 2023), Perceiver Resampler (Flamingo, Alayrac et al. 2022), and Perceiver IO (Jaegle et al. 2022). VTR is best understood as applying an established technique (learned cross-attention compression) motivated by a new diagnostic insight, not as a new architecture. The paper's literature map actually includes BLIP-2 and Flamingo but the paper text barely discusses the architectural relationship. This is a significant positioning gap.\n\n- **LIBERO-only evaluation is a serious limitation for CoRL.** CoRL reviewers typically expect either (a) real-robot experiments, or (b) evaluation across multiple simulation benchmarks (e.g., LIBERO + CALVIN + SimplerEnv). Single-benchmark simulation-only results will face pushback. The SmolVLA cliff-edge and VTR recovery findings would be far more compelling with at least one additional benchmark or a real-robot demonstration on even a single task.\n\n- **RoboFlamingo data quality is questionable.** The paper notes RoboFlamingo has a lower absolute baseline (42%) and acknowledges this may \"compress the dynamic range of degradation.\" The memory.md file reveals that RoboFlamingo training actually failed initially due to a \"gripper loss\" issue. The paper should more transparently discuss whether this baseline is reliable enough to anchor the \"gentle graded\" class. If the model is underperforming its expected capability, the pruning curve may not reflect the Perceiver Resampler's true behavior.\n\n- **pi0 is evaluated offline only.** The paper's most dramatic claim — that pi0 *improves* with 90% token removal — is based on normalized L1 loss, not closed-loop success rate. Offline loss metrics are known to be poorly correlated with closed-loop performance in manipulation (see diffusion policy literature). The paper acknowledges this limitation but still leads with pi0's immune status as the first class in the taxonomy. A closed-loop pi0 evaluation (even on a subset of tasks) would substantially strengthen this claim.\n\n- **The paper claims VTR is \"universal\" but tests it on only 2 of 4 connector types.** VTR is not tested on linear projection (pi0, X-VLA) or Perceiver Resampler (RoboFlamingo). The \"universal\" claim should be restricted to \"applicable across pixel-shuffle and MLP connectors.\" For linear projectors, VTR is unnecessary (they're already immune), but the claim of universality is still misleading.\n\n- **Pruning method inconsistency across models.** The paper uses random pruning for OpenVLA and L2-norm importance-based pruning for X-VLA. While Table 7 (pruning method comparison) shows these are roughly equivalent for OpenVLA, this equivalence is not verified for X-VLA. If importance-based pruning is actually *better* than random for X-VLA (which is plausible given its moderate CV), then the X-VLA cliff at k=0.7 might be even worse under random pruning. The paper's internal notes (D108) acknowledge this strengthens the findings, but the asymmetry should be more prominently discussed.\n\n- **Only 10 references in the bibliography.** This is well below the expected 25-40 references for a CoRL submission. The literature map file contains ~30 papers, but the actual paper cites only 10. Major omissions include: Perceiver/Perceiver IO (Jaegle et al. 2021, 2022), BLIP-2/Q-Former (Li et al. 2023), LLaVA (Liu et al. 2023), Flamingo (Alayrac et al. 2022), DynamicViT (Rao et al. 2021), EViT (Liang et al. 2022), Octo (Ghosh et al. 2024), VLA-Cache (Hsu et al. 2025), VLA-IAP (Wang et al. 2026), EfficientVLA (Yang et al. 2025), LLaVA-PruMerge (Shang et al. 2024), Open X-Embodiment (2024), pixel-shuffle origin paper (Shi et al. 2016), InternVL (Chen et al. 2024), OpenVLA-OFT itself, and DINOv2 (Oquab et al. 2024). All of these are in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The CV predictor has 5 data points and 4 proposed threshold bins. How would you validate this predictor on a new, unseen VLA architecture? Have you considered leave-one-out validation (e.g., predict X-VLA's behavior from the other 4 models' CV values)?\n\n2. Can you provide closed-loop pi0 evaluation on even 2-3 LIBERO tasks? The offline loss improvement at k=0.1 is counterintuitive and extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence. Is the improvement a regularization effect (fewer tokens = less overfitting to vision noise)?\n\n3. VTR on OpenVLA M=128 yields 0% SR due to severe overfitting. Given that VTR is architecturally identical to Q-Former/Perceiver Resampler (both learnable cross-attention compression), does this mean that adding a Q-Former *after* an MLP connector is fundamentally ill-conditioned? What do the VTC-only M=32 and M=64 results (noted as in-progress) show?\n\n4. For X-VLA, the cliff occurs at k=0.7 with enormous per-task variance ([94, 2, 100, 80, 28, 92, 12, 66, 0, 88]). This suggests some tasks are highly sensitive to token removal while others are not. Can you provide a per-task analysis relating task difficulty/visual complexity to pruning sensitivity? This would strengthen the mechanistic story.\n\n5. Why is RoboFlamingo's CV (0.11) lower than X-VLA's (0.151) but its pruning degradation appears worse in absolute terms (15% SR at k=0.5 vs 42.6%)? Does the lower baseline (42% vs 99.2%) confound the comparison? Would relative degradation be a fairer metric?\n\n6. The four concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) are mentioned but none are experimentally compared. For LightVLA (ICRA 2026, published) and See What Matters (ICML 2026, published), could you reproduce their method on SmolVLA to demonstrate the predicted failure?\n\n7. How does the CV metric relate to established information-theoretic measures like mutual information between tokens, or to linear probing accuracy on individual tokens? Is CV capturing something genuinely new or is it a proxy for token redundancy, which has been studied in the ViT efficiency literature?\n\n8. The paper frames VTR as adding ~22M parameters. For pi0 (3.3B) this is 0.7%, but for SmolVLA (450M) this is ~5%. At what point does the additional parameter cost negate the efficiency gains from compression?\n\n---\n\n### Novelty Score: 5/10\n\n**Justification:** The novelty is mixed. The cross-architecture analysis and the identification of connector type as the determining factor is a genuine contribution — no prior work has systematically compared token pruning across different VLA connector families. The SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is surprising and the controlled pi0-vs-OpenVLA comparison is well-designed. However, several components diminish overall novelty: (a) the CV predictor, while useful, is a straightforward statistical measure applied to 5 data points — the finding that higher heterogeneity implies higher vulnerability is expected from first principles; (b) VTR is architecturally identical to Q-Former/Perceiver, well-established in the VLM literature; (c) the taxonomy, while descriptive, is a categorization of observed behavior rather than a theoretical contribution; (d) the core insight — that the vision connector matters for compression — is likely unsurprising to architecture designers, even if the magnitude of the pixel-shuffle failure is surprising. The paper's novelty is primarily empirical (careful experiments revealing an important pattern) rather than conceptual or methodological.\n\n---\n\n### Significance Score: 6/10\n\n**Justification:** The work is relevant to the CoRL community. VLA deployment efficiency is a real concern, and the paper provides the first systematic guidance on which architectures tolerate pruning. The CV diagnostic is immediately actionable. The SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is a warning that could save practitioners significant debugging time. However, significance is limited by: (a) LIBERO-only evaluation without real-robot results; (b) the VTR method adds training cost, limiting its applicability for zero-shot VLA deployment; (c) the practical impact is partly pre-empted by the observation that linear-projection VLAs (pi0, X-VLA) are already efficient and don't need pruning, while pixel-shuffle VLAs (SmolVLA) need VTR which adds complexity. The \"sweet spot\" — MLP-projector VLAs where moderate pruning is viable — is already the target of 4 concurrent works. The paper's highest significance is as a diagnostic/explanatory contribution rather than a deployment solution.\n\n---\n\n### Related Work Score: 4/10\n\n**Justification:** The related work coverage is inadequate for a CoRL submission. Ten references is far too few. Critical gaps include:\n\n1. **Vision connector architectures:** No citation of Perceiver/Perceiver IO (Jaegle et al. 2021/2022), BLIP-2/Q-Former (Li et al. 2023), LLaVA (Liu et al. 2023), or Flamingo (Alayrac et al. 2022) — all directly relevant to the connector taxonomy and to VTR's architectural lineage.\n\n2. **ViT token efficiency:** No citation of DynamicViT (Rao et al. 2021), EViT (Liang et al. 2022), or LLaVA-PruMerge (Shang et al. 2024) — foundational works in the visual token pruning/merging literature that established the techniques the paper builds on.\n\n3. **VLA efficiency concurrent works:** VLA-IAP (Wang et al. 2026), VLA-Cache (Hsu et al. 2025), EfficientVLA (Yang et al. 2025), and VLA-Pruner (Liu et al. 2025) are either not cited or mentioned only in passing without experimental comparison. At minimum, the paper should discuss how its taxonomy predicts the success/failure of these methods.\n\n4. **VLA architectures:** Octo (Ghosh et al. 2024), Open X-Embodiment (2024), and OpenVLA-OFT itself lack citations despite OpenVLA-OFT being one of the 6 evaluated models.\n\n5. **Pixel-shuffle origin:** The pixel shuffle operation (Shi et al. 2016) that is central to the catastrophic failure finding is not cited.\n\n6. **Positioning vs concurrent work:** The paper lists 4 concurrent VLA pruning works but does not compare against any of them experimentally. For the two that are already published (LightVLA ICRA 2026, See What Matters ICML 2026), some experimental comparison or at minimum a detailed discussion of predicted outcomes is expected.\n\nThe project's own literature map contains ~30 well-curated references. The gap between the literature map and the actual paper citations suggests this is a drafting oversight rather than a knowledge gap, but it severely weakens the submission in its current form.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation\n\nThe paper addresses an important and timely question with a well-designed experimental study. The core finding — that connector architecture determines pruning tolerance — is sound and useful. However, the submission has three addressable-but-serious issues: (1) the related work section needs to at least double in size and properly cite foundational works on cross-attention compression, ViT token efficiency, and concurrent VLA efficiency methods; (2) the CV predictor claim needs to be modulated from \"continuous predictor\" to \"empirical trend observed across 5 architectures\"; and (3) evaluation limited to LIBERO simulation only will face resistance at CoRL without at least one additional benchmark or a real-robot probe experiment. With these addressed, the paper would be a solid accept at CoRL. In its current form, it is borderline."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa1d2550616220eb3 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa1d2550616220eb3' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 71340\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 147397</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"3f6a4487-fff4-4a03-8342-8910ece20526","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:40:44.638Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\nThe paper presents the first systematic cross-architecture study of visual token pruning robustness across 6 VLA models (π0, RoboFlamingo, X-VLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, SmolVLA) spanning 4 connector families (pure linear, Perceiver Resampler, linear+BART, MLP, pixel-shuffle). Key contributions:\n1. Discovery that the vision-to-LLM projection pipeline — not model size — determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum\n2. A four-class taxonomy: immune (CV=0.038), gentle graded (CV=0.11), threshold-graded (CV=0.151), smooth graded (CV=0.215), catastrophic (CV=0.314)\n3. Importance CV as a zero-cost continuous predictor\n4. Diagnose-then-Compress framework with VTR (~22M params cross-attention module)\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper\nSection 2.1 covers VLA models: RT-2 [1], OpenVLA [2], π0 [3], SmolVLA [4], X-VLA [5]\nSection 2.2 covers token reduction: FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, ToMe (token merging). Concurrent works: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, \"See What Matters\" — all propose pruning for MLP-projector VLAs.\n\nThe paper claims three differentiators: (1) explaining why pruning fails on pixel-shuffle, (2) identifying why pruning succeeds on linear projections, (3) proposing diagnostic-guided re-encoding motivated by cross-architecture analysis.\n\n## References (10 total)\n[1] Brohan et al. RT-2, CoRL 2023\n[2] Kim et al. OpenVLA, CoRL 2024\n[3] Black et al. π0, arXiv 2024\n[4] Zhu et al. SmolVLA, arXiv 2025\n[5] X-VLA Team. X-VLA, arXiv 2025\n[6] Li et al. VLMs as robot imitators, ICLR 2024\n[7] Liu et al. LIBERO, NeurIPS 2023\n[8] Chen et al. \"An image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2\", ECCV 2024\n[9] Zhang et al. H2O, NeurIPS 2023\n[10] Bolya et al. ToMe, ICLR 2023\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the cross-architecture pruning study? Has anyone done systematic comparisons before?\n- Is the CV-as-predictor finding genuinely novel or somewhat expected (higher CV → more heterogeneous → more vulnerable)?\n- Is the four-class taxonomy a genuine intellectual contribution or just a description of observed data?\n- Is VTR novel as a method, or is cross-attention re-encoding well-known in other contexts (e.g., Perceiver, Q-Former)?\n- How does this compare to concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters)?\n\n### 2. Significance for CoRL Community\n- Is this relevant to the robot learning community specifically, or is it more of a VLM efficiency paper?\n- Does the paper provide actionable guidance for practitioners deploying VLAs?\n- Would reviewers at CoRL value the diagnostic (CV) tool?\n- Is the LIBERO-only evaluation sufficient for a robotics venue, or would real-robot experiments be expected?\n\n### 3. Related Work Completeness\n- Only 10 references — is this sufficient for a CoRL submission?\n- Are important related works missing? Consider: Perceiver/Perceiver IO, Q-Former/BLIP-2, FlexAttention, visual token compression in VLMs more broadly, other VLA architectures (Octo, RT-H, etc.)\n- The concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) are mentioned but not compared experimentally. Is this a gap?\n- Is the positioning relative to token merging methods (ToMe) adequate? Only mentioned briefly.\n\n### 4. Writing and Presentation\n- Is the narrative structure (diagnosis → taxonomy → method) effective?\n- Are claims well-calibrated or overclaimed?\n- The title is attention-grabbing — does the content deliver?\n\n### 5. Specific Concerns\n- The paper claims \"first systematic cross-architecture study\" — verify if this is actually true\n- The CV predictor is validated on only 5-6 data points (one per model). Is this sufficient to claim it's a \"continuous predictor\"?\n- VTR is called \"universal\" but only tested on 2 of 4 connector types\n- No real-robot experiments — how critical is this for CoRL?\n\nFormat your review as:\n**Strengths** (bulleted list)\n**Weaknesses** (bulleted list)\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n**Novelty Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Significance Score** (1-10, with justification)\n**Related Work Score** (1-10, with justification)","agentId":"aa1d2550616220eb3","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, its experimental evidence, the project's internal decision log, the full paper tex, the literature map, and the framing document. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review of \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work/Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined research question with clean experimental design.** The paper asks a specific, answerable question — why does token pruning work on some VLAs but not others? — and systematically tests it across 6 models. The controlled comparison between pi0 (256 tokens, linear) and OpenVLA (256 tokens, MLP) is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper, genuinely isolating connector type from model size and token count.\n\n- **The four-class taxonomy is descriptively useful.** Even if the taxonomy is ultimately empirical, it provides the community with a concrete vocabulary and reference table for reasoning about token compression across architectures. The progression from immune to catastrophic, with two intermediate classes, is more nuanced than a simple binary \"works/doesn't work.\"\n\n- **Actionable practitioner guidance.** The CV diagnostic (one forward pass, zero cost) is immediately usable. A robotics engineer considering token pruning for a new VLA deployment can compute CV and get a calibrated expectation of pruning tolerance. This is the kind of tool CoRL reviewers value.\n\n- **SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is striking.** The demonstration that removing just 5% of tokens (7/128) from a pixel-shuffle connector yields 0% SR is a genuinely surprising and important negative result. The fact that this holds across all pruning strategies (random, ACIS, ToMe, geometric priors) and across task suites makes it robust.\n\n- **VTR recovery from 0% to 60%+ on pixel-shuffle is convincing.** The flat M-curve (M=16 through M=128 all within ±5pp) combined with architecture independence (cross-attention vs MLP adapter, p=0.15) provides strong evidence that re-encoding is the causal mechanism, not a specific architecture or compression ratio.\n\n- **Honest treatment of negative results.** The paper reports VTR's failure on OpenVLA MLP projector (0% SR at M=128) and reframes it as a connector-dependency finding rather than hiding it. The OFT eval bug is acknowledged. This builds credibility.\n\n- **RoboFlamingo inclusion fills a gap.** Adding the Perceiver Resampler as a \"gentle graded\" class (CV=0.11) strengthens the continuous spectrum narrative beyond the original 3-class structure.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **CV as \"continuous predictor\" is validated on only 5 data points.** The paper claims CV forms a continuous spectrum predicting pruning tolerance, but this is fitted to exactly 5 models (pi0=0.038, RoboFlamingo=0.11, X-VLA=0.151, OpenVLA=0.215, SmolVLA=0.314). With 5 data points, any monotonic function would appear to \"predict\" behavior. The thresholds proposed (CV<0.05 = immune, 0.05-0.15 = gentle, etc.) are post-hoc bin boundaries drawn around individual data points rather than validated decision boundaries. This is the paper's most overclaimed contribution. At minimum, the paper should acknowledge this is a hypothesis generated from 5 observations, not a validated predictor.\n\n- **\"First systematic cross-architecture study\" claim needs qualification.** The claim is likely true in the narrow sense of token *pruning* across VLA architectures specifically. However, cross-architecture efficiency comparisons in VLMs more broadly are not new — EfficientVLA (Yang et al. 2025) tests on CogACT, LLaVA-PruMerge tests across LLaVA variants, and the RoboVLMs framework (2024) systematically compares 8+ VLM backbones. The paper should sharpen this claim to \"first study specifically isolating the connector's role in determining pruning robustness.\"\n\n- **VTR is not architecturally novel.** Cross-attention re-encoding with learnable queries is precisely the mechanism of Q-Former (BLIP-2, Li et al. 2023), Perceiver Resampler (Flamingo, Alayrac et al. 2022), and Perceiver IO (Jaegle et al. 2022). VTR is best understood as applying an established technique (learned cross-attention compression) motivated by a new diagnostic insight, not as a new architecture. The paper's literature map actually includes BLIP-2 and Flamingo but the paper text barely discusses the architectural relationship. This is a significant positioning gap.\n\n- **LIBERO-only evaluation is a serious limitation for CoRL.** CoRL reviewers typically expect either (a) real-robot experiments, or (b) evaluation across multiple simulation benchmarks (e.g., LIBERO + CALVIN + SimplerEnv). Single-benchmark simulation-only results will face pushback. The SmolVLA cliff-edge and VTR recovery findings would be far more compelling with at least one additional benchmark or a real-robot demonstration on even a single task.\n\n- **RoboFlamingo data quality is questionable.** The paper notes RoboFlamingo has a lower absolute baseline (42%) and acknowledges this may \"compress the dynamic range of degradation.\" The memory.md file reveals that RoboFlamingo training actually failed initially due to a \"gripper loss\" issue. The paper should more transparently discuss whether this baseline is reliable enough to anchor the \"gentle graded\" class. If the model is underperforming its expected capability, the pruning curve may not reflect the Perceiver Resampler's true behavior.\n\n- **pi0 is evaluated offline only.** The paper's most dramatic claim — that pi0 *improves* with 90% token removal — is based on normalized L1 loss, not closed-loop success rate. Offline loss metrics are known to be poorly correlated with closed-loop performance in manipulation (see diffusion policy literature). The paper acknowledges this limitation but still leads with pi0's immune status as the first class in the taxonomy. A closed-loop pi0 evaluation (even on a subset of tasks) would substantially strengthen this claim.\n\n- **The paper claims VTR is \"universal\" but tests it on only 2 of 4 connector types.** VTR is not tested on linear projection (pi0, X-VLA) or Perceiver Resampler (RoboFlamingo). The \"universal\" claim should be restricted to \"applicable across pixel-shuffle and MLP connectors.\" For linear projectors, VTR is unnecessary (they're already immune), but the claim of universality is still misleading.\n\n- **Pruning method inconsistency across models.** The paper uses random pruning for OpenVLA and L2-norm importance-based pruning for X-VLA. While Table 7 (pruning method comparison) shows these are roughly equivalent for OpenVLA, this equivalence is not verified for X-VLA. If importance-based pruning is actually *better* than random for X-VLA (which is plausible given its moderate CV), then the X-VLA cliff at k=0.7 might be even worse under random pruning. The paper's internal notes (D108) acknowledge this strengthens the findings, but the asymmetry should be more prominently discussed.\n\n- **Only 10 references in the bibliography.** This is well below the expected 25-40 references for a CoRL submission. The literature map file contains ~30 papers, but the actual paper cites only 10. Major omissions include: Perceiver/Perceiver IO (Jaegle et al. 2021, 2022), BLIP-2/Q-Former (Li et al. 2023), LLaVA (Liu et al. 2023), Flamingo (Alayrac et al. 2022), DynamicViT (Rao et al. 2021), EViT (Liang et al. 2022), Octo (Ghosh et al. 2024), VLA-Cache (Hsu et al. 2025), VLA-IAP (Wang et al. 2026), EfficientVLA (Yang et al. 2025), LLaVA-PruMerge (Shang et al. 2024), Open X-Embodiment (2024), pixel-shuffle origin paper (Shi et al. 2016), InternVL (Chen et al. 2024), OpenVLA-OFT itself, and DINOv2 (Oquab et al. 2024). All of these are in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The CV predictor has 5 data points and 4 proposed threshold bins. How would you validate this predictor on a new, unseen VLA architecture? Have you considered leave-one-out validation (e.g., predict X-VLA's behavior from the other 4 models' CV values)?\n\n2. Can you provide closed-loop pi0 evaluation on even 2-3 LIBERO tasks? The offline loss improvement at k=0.1 is counterintuitive and extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence. Is the improvement a regularization effect (fewer tokens = less overfitting to vision noise)?\n\n3. VTR on OpenVLA M=128 yields 0% SR due to severe overfitting. Given that VTR is architecturally identical to Q-Former/Perceiver Resampler (both learnable cross-attention compression), does this mean that adding a Q-Former *after* an MLP connector is fundamentally ill-conditioned? What do the VTC-only M=32 and M=64 results (noted as in-progress) show?\n\n4. For X-VLA, the cliff occurs at k=0.7 with enormous per-task variance ([94, 2, 100, 80, 28, 92, 12, 66, 0, 88]). This suggests some tasks are highly sensitive to token removal while others are not. Can you provide a per-task analysis relating task difficulty/visual complexity to pruning sensitivity? This would strengthen the mechanistic story.\n\n5. Why is RoboFlamingo's CV (0.11) lower than X-VLA's (0.151) but its pruning degradation appears worse in absolute terms (15% SR at k=0.5 vs 42.6%)? Does the lower baseline (42% vs 99.2%) confound the comparison? Would relative degradation be a fairer metric?\n\n6. The four concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters) are mentioned but none are experimentally compared. For LightVLA (ICRA 2026, published) and See What Matters (ICML 2026, published), could you reproduce their method on SmolVLA to demonstrate the predicted failure?\n\n7. How does the CV metric relate to established information-theoretic measures like mutual information between tokens, or to linear probing accuracy on individual tokens? Is CV capturing something genuinely new or is it a proxy for token redundancy, which has been studied in the ViT efficiency literature?\n\n8. The paper frames VTR as adding ~22M parameters. For pi0 (3.3B) this is 0.7%, but for SmolVLA (450M) this is ~5%. At what point does the additional parameter cost negate the efficiency gains from compression?\n\n---\n\n### Novelty Score: 5/10\n\n**Justification:** The novelty is mixed. The cross-architecture analysis and the identification of connector type as the determining factor is a genuine contribution — no prior work has systematically compared token pruning across different VLA connector families. The SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is surprising and the controlled pi0-vs-OpenVLA comparison is well-designed. However, several components diminish overall novelty: (a) the CV predictor, while useful, is a straightforward statistical measure applied to 5 data points — the finding that higher heterogeneity implies higher vulnerability is expected from first principles; (b) VTR is architecturally identical to Q-Former/Perceiver, well-established in the VLM literature; (c) the taxonomy, while descriptive, is a categorization of observed behavior rather than a theoretical contribution; (d) the core insight — that the vision connector matters for compression — is likely unsurprising to architecture designers, even if the magnitude of the pixel-shuffle failure is surprising. The paper's novelty is primarily empirical (careful experiments revealing an important pattern) rather than conceptual or methodological.\n\n---\n\n### Significance Score: 6/10\n\n**Justification:** The work is relevant to the CoRL community. VLA deployment efficiency is a real concern, and the paper provides the first systematic guidance on which architectures tolerate pruning. The CV diagnostic is immediately actionable. The SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is a warning that could save practitioners significant debugging time. However, significance is limited by: (a) LIBERO-only evaluation without real-robot results; (b) the VTR method adds training cost, limiting its applicability for zero-shot VLA deployment; (c) the practical impact is partly pre-empted by the observation that linear-projection VLAs (pi0, X-VLA) are already efficient and don't need pruning, while pixel-shuffle VLAs (SmolVLA) need VTR which adds complexity. The \"sweet spot\" — MLP-projector VLAs where moderate pruning is viable — is already the target of 4 concurrent works. The paper's highest significance is as a diagnostic/explanatory contribution rather than a deployment solution.\n\n---\n\n### Related Work Score: 4/10\n\n**Justification:** The related work coverage is inadequate for a CoRL submission. Ten references is far too few. Critical gaps include:\n\n1. **Vision connector architectures:** No citation of Perceiver/Perceiver IO (Jaegle et al. 2021/2022), BLIP-2/Q-Former (Li et al. 2023), LLaVA (Liu et al. 2023), or Flamingo (Alayrac et al. 2022) — all directly relevant to the connector taxonomy and to VTR's architectural lineage.\n\n2. **ViT token efficiency:** No citation of DynamicViT (Rao et al. 2021), EViT (Liang et al. 2022), or LLaVA-PruMerge (Shang et al. 2024) — foundational works in the visual token pruning/merging literature that established the techniques the paper builds on.\n\n3. **VLA efficiency concurrent works:** VLA-IAP (Wang et al. 2026), VLA-Cache (Hsu et al. 2025), EfficientVLA (Yang et al. 2025), and VLA-Pruner (Liu et al. 2025) are either not cited or mentioned only in passing without experimental comparison. At minimum, the paper should discuss how its taxonomy predicts the success/failure of these methods.\n\n4. **VLA architectures:** Octo (Ghosh et al. 2024), Open X-Embodiment (2024), and OpenVLA-OFT itself lack citations despite OpenVLA-OFT being one of the 6 evaluated models.\n\n5. **Pixel-shuffle origin:** The pixel shuffle operation (Shi et al. 2016) that is central to the catastrophic failure finding is not cited.\n\n6. **Positioning vs concurrent work:** The paper lists 4 concurrent VLA pruning works but does not compare against any of them experimentally. For the two that are already published (LightVLA ICRA 2026, See What Matters ICML 2026), some experimental comparison or at minimum a detailed discussion of predicted outcomes is expected.\n\nThe project's own literature map contains ~30 well-curated references. The gap between the literature map and the actual paper citations suggests this is a drafting oversight rather than a knowledge gap, but it severely weakens the submission in its current form.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation\n\nThe paper addresses an important and timely question with a well-designed experimental study. The core finding — that connector architecture determines pruning tolerance — is sound and useful. However, the submission has three addressable-but-serious issues: (1) the related work section needs to at least double in size and properly cite foundational works on cross-attention compression, ViT token efficiency, and concurrent VLA efficiency methods; (2) the CV predictor claim needs to be modulated from \"continuous predictor\" to \"empirical trend observed across 5 architectures\"; and (3) evaluation limited to LIBERO simulation only will face resistance at CoRL without at least one additional benchmark or a real-robot probe experiment. With these addressed, the paper would be a solid accept at CoRL. In its current form, it is borderline."}],"totalDurationMs":147397,"totalTokens":71340,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":295,"cache_read_input_tokens":67062,"output_tokens":3982,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":295},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3982,"cache_read_input_tokens":67062,"cache_creation_input_tokens":295,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":295,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QuYQBLZseRZcP8PEJPmNLm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews (done - R1 and R2 above)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing - share each reviewer's findings with the other\nStep 3: Convergence - identify areas of agreement/disagreement\nStep 4: Synthesis - final consolidated report\n\nLet me now proceed with the roundtable Steps 2-4 and then submit the final review report.\n\nLet me synthesize the reviews into a final report. The roundtable process:\n\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing**\nR1 (Methods+Experiments) key points:\n\n\nR1 rated methods and experiments at 6/10 each, highlighting the cross-architecture design and rigorous ablation of SmolVLA as strengths, along with the true removal protocol and isolated VTR mechanism testing. However, they flagged concerns about heterogeneous evaluation modes, limited seed robustness, RoboFlamingo data quality issues, an overclaimed \"universal\" VTR claim, OpenVLA's single-seed validation, OFT's lack of pruning contribution, and an unexplained performance dip at M=96.\n\nR2 (Novelty+Related Work) key points:\nR2 assessed novelty at 5/10 and significance at 6/10, with related work at 4/10, appreciating the well-defined research question and practical guidance for practitioners, plus the SmolVLA cliff-edge finding and VTR recovery results, though noting some honest negative results.\n\nWeaknesses from R2 include the CV predictor relying on only 5 data points, VTR lacking architectural novelty since it's essentially Q-Former, evaluation limited to LIBERO, sparse references (10 total), and no experimental comparison against concurrent work.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on several key issues: the cross-architecture study design stands as the paper's strongest element, the SmolVLA cliff-edge is well-established and surprising, the CV predictor is useful but overstated given its limited data, the \"universal\" VTR claim overreaches with only 2 connector types tested, pi0's offline-only evaluation is a major limitation, RoboFlamingo's data quality is questionable, and seed robustness remains thin. They diverge mainly on emphasis, with R2 placing greater weight on related work gaps.\n\nNow I'm synthesizing the overall assessment: the paper delivers solid empirical contributions through its cross-architecture study and the SmolVLA cliff-edge discovery, but it's hampered by overclaimed results on the CV predictor, inadequate related work coverage, LIBERO-only evaluation scope, heterogeneous evaluation modes, and insufficient seed robustness analysis. For CoRL standards, this lands in borderline territory, so I need to map this against their scoring rubric to determine the final rating.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10501,"cache_read_input_tokens":52393,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10501},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cecc2908-68ca-4714-bd9d-0379a2878afb","uuid":"67578d53-c171-4e15-abc2-11dac05a7a7c"}
